window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-photosynthesis","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants · Practice","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of the bundle sheath cells of C4 plants?","image":"","options":["They contain a large number of chloroplasts","Their walls are thick and impervious to gaseous exchange","They may form several layers around the vascular bundle","They have large intercellular spaces for $CO_2$ diffusion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes C4 bundle sheath cells as follows:\n(1) They have a large number of chloroplasts. Correct.\n(2) They have thick walls that are impervious to gaseous exchange. Correct.\n(3) They may form several layers around the vascular bundle. Correct.\n(4) Incorrect. These cells have NO intercellular spaces. $CO_2$ reaches them as C4 acids, not by diffusion through air spaces.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which pigment acts directly to convert light energy to chemical energy?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a","Chlorophyll b","Xanthophyll","Carotenoid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a makes up the reaction centres P680 (PSII) and P700 (PSI). Here absorbed light drives electron transfer, which turns light energy into chemical energy.\nChlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids are accessory pigments. They widen the range of light absorbed and pass the energy on to chlorophyll a, but they do not convert it directly.\nChlorophyll b is tempting because it is also a chlorophyll, but it only harvests light.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In purple and green sulphur bacteria, H$_2$S acts as the hydrogen donor in photosynthesis. Based on van Niel's conclusions, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["These bacteria release O$_2$, which comes from CO$_2$","These bacteria release sulphur or sulphate instead of O$_2$, which suggests that the O$_2$ released by green plants comes from water","These bacteria release O$_2$ from H$_2$S, so in green plants O$_2$ must come from CO$_2$","These bacteria do not reduce CO$_2$ to carbohydrate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"van Niel's general equation is $2H_2A + CO_2 \\rightarrow 2A + CH_2O + H_2O$. Here $H_2A$ is the hydrogen donor and is oxidised.\nWhen $H_2A$ is H$_2$S, the oxidation product is sulphur or sulphate, not O$_2$. CO$_2$ is still reduced to carbohydrate.\nBy analogy, in green plants $H_2A$ is H$_2$O. The O$_2$ released therefore comes from water, not from CO$_2$. Radioisotope techniques later confirmed this.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Light of wavelengths only beyond 680 nm can drive non-cyclic photophosphorylation.\nStatement II: The reaction centre of PS I can be excited by red light of 700 nm.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Non-cyclic flow needs PS II (P680) and PS I to work in series. NCERT states that when only light beyond 680 nm is available, cyclic photophosphorylation occurs instead.\nStatement II is correct. P700 absorbs at 700 nm, and its electrons are excited by this red light.\nSo PS I can still work in far-red light, but PS II cannot.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The reaction centre chlorophyll a of Photosystem I has its absorption peak at","image":"","options":["680 nm","700 nm","660 nm","720 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each photosystem has a reaction centre chlorophyll a with a specific absorption maximum.\nIn PS I, the peak is at 700 nm, so the reaction centre is called P700.\n680 nm is the tempting wrong choice. That value belongs to the PS II reaction centre (P680).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are true for $C_4$ plants such as maize and sorghum?\n(i) Their leaves show Kranz anatomy.\n(ii) Their mesophyll cells lack RuBisCO.\n(iii) Their bundle sheath cells have thin walls and many intercellular spaces.\n(iv) Two cell types in the leaf take part in fixing $CO_2$.\n(v) They show high photorespiration at high light intensity.\n(vi) They tolerate higher temperatures than $C_3$ plants.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) True: they have a wreath-like ring of large bundle sheath cells (Kranz anatomy).\n(ii) True: their mesophyll cells have PEPcase but no RuBisCO.\n(iii) False: bundle sheath walls are thick, impervious to gases, and have no intercellular spaces.\n(iv) True: both mesophyll and bundle sheath cells fix $CO_2$.\n(v) False: $C_4$ plants lack photorespiration.\n(vi) True: they tolerate higher temperatures.\nSo four statements are true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: One reason protons accumulate in the thylakoid lumen is that NADP reductase removes protons from the lumen while reducing $NADP^+$ to NADPH + $H^+$.\nStatement II: In photosynthesis protons accumulate inside the thylakoid lumen, whereas in respiration protons accumulate in the intermembrane space of mitochondria.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. NADP reductase is located on the stroma side and removes protons from the stroma, not the lumen. This lowers stromal proton concentration and so adds to the gradient.\nLumen protons actually come from water splitting on the inner side and from H-carriers that pick up stromal protons and release them inside.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT names this as the one key difference from mitochondrial chemiosmosis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose an illuminated chloroplast is treated with a chemical that makes the thylakoid membrane freely leaky to protons. Electron flow from water to NADP$^+$ still continues. Which outcome is most expected?","image":"","options":["Lumen pH drops even further, so ATP synthesis increases","ATP synthesis through ATP synthase falls sharply, because a proton gradient cannot be maintained","Water splitting stops, so no O$_2$ is released","NADP$^+$ can no longer be reduced, because NADP reductase needs ATP"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ATP synthase makes ATP only when protons flow down a gradient through CF$_0$.\nA leaky membrane lets protons equalise across it, so no gradient or pH difference can build up.\nWithout a gradient, CF$_1$ gets no energy for its conformational change, so ATP synthesis falls.\nWater splitting and NADPH formation depend on electron flow, which is stated to continue, so (3) and (4) are wrong.\nThe lumen pH cannot drop further when protons leak out, so (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A hypothetical mutant plant has normal chlorophyll a but lacks all accessory pigments. Compared with a normal plant under the same conditions, which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["Wider range of usable wavelengths and lower risk of photo-oxidation of chlorophyll a","Narrower range of usable wavelengths and greater risk of photo-oxidation of chlorophyll a","No photosynthesis at all, since accessory pigments form the reaction centres","No change, since accessory pigments only give colour to the leaf"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Accessory pigments have two roles. They let a wider range of wavelengths be used, and they protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.\nWithout them, the plant can use fewer wavelengths and chlorophyll a is more exposed to photo-oxidation, so option 2 fits.\nOption 3 fails because both reaction centres (P680 and P700) are chlorophyll a, which this plant still has, so it can still photosynthesise.\nOption 4 ignores the functional roles of these pigments.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a paper chromatogram of the pigments of a green leaf, which pigment appears as a yellow-green band?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a","Chlorophyll b","Xanthophylls","Carotenoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Paper chromatography separates leaf pigments into four bands.\nChlorophyll b appears yellow green.\nChlorophyll a is bright or blue green, xanthophylls are yellow, and carotenoids are yellow to yellow-orange.\nThe tempting choice, chlorophyll a, is green but bluish rather than yellowish.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a paper chromatogram of leaf pigments, chlorophyll b appears yellow-green.\nStatement II: Accessory pigments such as carotenoids protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On a chromatogram, chlorophyll a is bright or blue-green and chlorophyll b is yellow-green. Xanthophylls are yellow, and carotenoids are yellow to yellow-orange.\nStatement II is correct. Accessory pigments let a wider range of wavelengths be used. They also protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Blackman's Law of Limiting Factors (1905) states that when a chemical process is affected by more than one factor, its rate is determined by the factor that is:","image":"","options":["nearest to its minimal value","present in the maximum amount","nearest to its optimum value","least affected by temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Blackman's law says the rate is set by the factor nearest to its minimal value.\nThat factor directly changes the rate when its quantity is changed.\nA factor present in excess or at its optimum does not limit the rate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In C4 plants, the Calvin cycle does not take place in the mesophyll cells.\nReason (R): The mesophyll cells of C4 plants lack the enzyme RuBisCO.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In C4 plants, the Calvin cycle runs only in the bundle sheath cells. In C3 plants, by contrast, it runs in all mesophyll cells.\nR is true. NCERT stresses that C4 mesophyll cells lack RuBisCO and contain PEPcase instead.\nRuBisCO catalyses the carboxylation step of the Calvin cycle. Without RuBisCO, the cycle cannot operate in the mesophyll. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In C4 plants, the Calvin cycle does not take place in the mesophyll cells and occurs only in the bundle sheath cells.\nReason (R): Bundle sheath cells of C4 plants are rich in RuBisCO, whereas mesophyll cells lack this enzyme.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In C4 plants the Calvin pathway runs only in the bundle sheath cells. In C3 plants it runs in all mesophyll cells.\nR is true. RuBisCO is plentiful in the bundle sheath cells and absent from the mesophyll cells.\nRuBisCO catalyses carboxylation of RuBP, the first step of the Calvin cycle. So the cycle can only operate where this enzyme is present. R therefore explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Hatch and Slack pathway, which molecule acts as the primary acceptor of $CO_2$ in the mesophyll cells of a C4 plant?","image":"","options":["Ribulose bisphosphate (5C)","Phosphoenol pyruvate (3C)","Oxaloacetic acid (4C)","3-phosphoglyceric acid (3C)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In C4 plants, the first $CO_2$ fixation occurs in the mesophyll cells.\nThe acceptor there is phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP), a 3-carbon molecule. The enzyme PEP carboxylase (PEPcase) catalyses the fixation.\nRuBP is the acceptor in the Calvin cycle, which runs in the bundle sheath cells, not the mesophyll.\nOAA is the product of the fixation, not the acceptor. PGA is the first product in C3 plants.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following events help build up the proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane during the light reaction?\n(i) Splitting of water on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane\n(ii) The primary electron acceptor, located towards the outer side of the membrane, passing its electron to a hydrogen carrier that picks up a proton from the stroma\n(iii) Reduction of $NADP^+$ by NADP reductase on the stroma side of the membrane\n(iv) Movement of protons through the $CF_0$ channel of ATP synthase into the stroma\n(v) Diffusion of $O_2$ out of the chloroplast","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Builds the gradient: the protons released by water splitting stay in the lumen.\n(ii) Builds the gradient: the H-carrier takes a proton from the stroma and releases it into the lumen.\n(iii) Builds the gradient: reducing $NADP^+$ uses up protons from the stroma.\n(iv) Does not build the gradient: protons flowing back through $CF_0$ break the gradient down to make ATP.\n(v) Does not build the gradient: release of $O_2$ does not move protons.\nSo three events build the gradient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"According to the chemiosmotic hypothesis for photosynthesis, where do protons accumulate when electrons move through the photosystems?","image":"","options":["In the stroma of the chloroplast","In the lumen of the thylakoid","In the space between the outer and inner chloroplast membranes","In the cytoplasm outside the chloroplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In chloroplasts, the proton gradient forms across the thylakoid membrane.\nProtons build up towards the inside of this membrane, that is, in the thylakoid lumen.\nThe stroma actually loses protons, so option (1) is the reverse of what happens.\nAn intermembrane-space build-up is the mitochondrial pattern, not the chloroplast one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a feature of the bundle sheath cells in the Kranz anatomy of maize leaves?","image":"","options":["They contain a large number of chloroplasts","They have thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange","They have abundant intercellular spaces for CO$_2$ diffusion","They are rich in RuBisCO but lack PEPcase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes Kranz bundle sheath cells as large and rich in chloroplasts. They have thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.\nThese cells carry out the Calvin cycle, so they have RuBisCO but lack PEPcase.\nAbundant intercellular spaces would let the released CO$_2$ escape. This defeats the CO$_2$-concentrating role, so option (3) is not a feature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The membrane system of the chloroplast is responsible for trapping light energy and for synthesising ATP and NADPH.\nStatement II: Starch is synthesised within the thylakoid lumen from the sugar made there.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The grana and stroma lamellae membranes trap light energy and form ATP and NADPH.\nStatement II is incorrect. Sugar synthesis takes place through enzymatic reactions in the stroma, and starch forms there too, as starch granules.\nThe thylakoid lumen is the space where protons accumulate during the light reaction; sugar is not made there.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Isolated chloroplasts are illuminated only with light of wavelength 700 nm. All other conditions are suitable. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["Both ATP and NADPH are formed, and O$_2$ is evolved","ATP is formed, but NADPH is not formed and no O$_2$ is evolved","NADPH is formed, but ATP is not formed","Neither ATP nor NADPH is formed because P700 cannot be excited"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Light of 700 nm can excite P700 of PS I. It is beyond 680 nm, so PS II (P680) is not driven effectively.\nNCERT notes that cyclic photophosphorylation occurs when only light beyond 680 nm is available.\nCyclic flow gives ATP only. No electron reaches NADP$^+$, so no NADPH forms.\nWater splitting is linked to PS II, so no O2 is evolved.\nOption (4) fails because P700 absorbs best at 700 nm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) An absorption spectrum shows how much light a pigment absorbs at different wavelengths, while an action spectrum shows the rate of photosynthesis at different wavelengths.\n(ii) The action spectrum of photosynthesis overlaps the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a exactly at every wavelength.\n(iii) Chlorophyll a shows its absorption peaks in the blue and red regions of the spectrum.\n(iv) Carotenoids help protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.\n(v) Accessory pigments convert absorbed light directly into chemical energy without involving chlorophyll a.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition of each spectrum.\n(ii) Incorrect: the overlap is not one-to-one, because photosynthesis also occurs at wavelengths that chlorophyll a absorbs poorly.\n(iii) Correct: chlorophyll a absorbs most strongly in blue and red light, where the rate of photosynthesis is also higher.\n(iv) Correct: accessory pigments such as carotenoids protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.\n(v) Incorrect: accessory pigments pass their absorbed energy to chlorophyll a.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a shows its maximum absorption in which regions of the visible spectrum?","image":"","options":["Green and yellow","Blue and red","Violet and green","Orange and yellow"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a has two peaks: one in the blue region and one in the red region.\nThe rate of photosynthesis is also higher at these wavelengths.\nAbsorption is low in the green region, which is why leaves look green.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A graph that plots the rate of photosynthesis against the wavelength of light used is called:","image":"","options":["Absorption spectrum","Emission spectrum","Action spectrum","Chromatogram"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An action spectrum shows how effectively each wavelength drives a process, here measured as the rate of photosynthesis.\nAn absorption spectrum shows how much light a pigment absorbs at each wavelength. It measures absorption, not the rate of a process.\nEngelmann described the first action spectrum of photosynthesis using Cladophora and aerobic bacteria.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The light harvesting complexes are made up of hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins.\nStatement II: The reaction centre chlorophyll a is identical in PS I and PS II and absorbs maximally at the same wavelength.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes the LHC as hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins.\nStatement II is incorrect. The reaction centre is different in the two photosystems.\nThe PS I reaction centre absorbs at 700 nm (P700), while the PS II reaction centre absorbs at 680 nm (P680).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the leaf pigments separated by paper chromatography (List-I) with their colour on the chromatogram (List-II).\nList-I:\n(a) Chlorophyll a\n(b) Chlorophyll b\n(c) Xanthophylls\n(d) Carotenoids\nList-II:\n(i) Yellow\n(ii) Yellow to yellow-orange\n(iii) Bright or blue green\n(iv) Yellow green","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Chlorophyll a appears bright or blue green, so a-iii.\n(b) Chlorophyll b appears yellow green, so b-iv.\n(c) Xanthophylls appear yellow, so c-i.\n(d) Carotenoids appear yellow to yellow-orange, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which enzyme does NCERT describe as the most abundant enzyme in the world? Its active site can bind both $CO_2$ and $O_2$.","image":"","options":["PEP carboxylase","ATP synthase","Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase","NADP reductase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"RuBisCO (RuBP carboxylase-oxygenase) is the most abundant enzyme in the world.\nIts active site can bind either $CO_2$ (carboxylation) or $O_2$ (oxygenation), which is why it carries both names.\nPEP carboxylase fixes $CO_2$ in $C_4$ mesophyll cells, but it has no oxygenase activity.\nATP synthase and NADP reductase work in the light reaction, not in fixing $CO_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Water-splitting complex\n(b) NADP reductase\n(c) CF$_0$\n(d) CF$_1$\nList-II\n(i) Transmembrane channel within the thylakoid membrane\n(ii) Associated with the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane\n(iii) On the stroma side of the membrane; consumes stromal protons\n(iv) Projects into the stroma; its conformational change makes ATP","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Water splitting occurs on the inner side of the membrane, releasing protons into the lumen, so a-ii.\n(b) NADP reductase is on the stroma side and uses stromal protons, so b-iii.\n(c) CF$_0$ is the channel embedded in the membrane, so c-i.\n(d) CF$_1$ faces the stroma and changes conformation to synthesise ATP, so d-iv.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage of the Calvin cycle) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(a) Carboxylation\n(b) Reduction\n(c) Regeneration\n(d) Overall requirement per $CO_2$ fixed\nList-II:\n(i) 3 ATP and 2 NADPH\n(ii) 1 ATP used to form RuBP\n(iii) 2 ATP and 2 NADPH used, leading to sugar formation\n(iv) RuBisCO forms two molecules of 3-PGA","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Carboxylation: RuBisCO adds $CO_2$ to RuBP, giving two molecules of 3-PGA, so (iv).\n(b) Reduction: 2 ATP and 2 NADPH are used per $CO_2$, leading to sugar formation, so (iii).\n(c) Regeneration: 1 ATP is used to re-form RuBP, so (ii).\n(d) Overall: 2 + 1 = 3 ATP and 2 NADPH per $CO_2$, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The CF$_1$ portion of the chloroplast ATP synthase is located","image":"","options":["embedded within the thylakoid membrane as a proton channel","on the inner surface of the thylakoid membrane, facing the lumen","protruding from the outer surface of the thylakoid membrane, facing the stroma","on the inner membrane of the chloroplast envelope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"ATP synthase has two parts.\nCF$_0$ is embedded in the thylakoid membrane and forms the transmembrane channel.\nCF$_1$ protrudes on the outer surface of the thylakoid membrane, on the side facing the stroma.\nThis is why ATP is released into the stroma, where the Calvin cycle uses it.\nOption (1) describes CF$_0$, not CF$_1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which metal ion is a constituent of chlorophyll?","image":"","options":["Iron","Copper","Magnesium","Zinc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll has a porphyrin ring (head) with a single metal ion held at its centre. That ion is magnesium (Mg$^{2+}$).\nThis is why magnesium deficiency causes chlorosis, the yellowing of leaves.\nIron is the tempting wrong option. Iron is needed to synthesise chlorophyll and is found in cytochromes, but it is not part of the chlorophyll molecule. Copper and zinc are also not constituents.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The characteristic 'Z' shape of the electron transport scheme in the light reaction appears when all the electron carriers are arranged in sequence on a:","image":"","options":["Wavelength (absorption) scale","pH scale of the thylakoid lumen","Temperature scale","Redox potential scale"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The Z scheme traces the electron's path in order:\nfrom PS II, uphill to an acceptor;\ndown the electron transport chain to PS I;\nuphill again on excitation at PS I;\nfinally downhill to NADP$^+$.\nThis zig-zag forms a 'Z' only when the carriers are plotted on a redox (oxidation-reduction) potential scale.\nThe wavelength option is tempting because P680 and P700 are named after wavelengths. However, wavelengths only identify the reaction centres; they do not give the Z its shape.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (chloroplast component) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\nA. Grana\nB. Stroma lamellae\nC. Stroma\nD. Starch granule\nList-II\n(i) Site of enzymatic synthesis of sugar\n(ii) Storage form of the carbohydrate made in the chloroplast\n(iii) Membranes having both PS I and PS II\n(iv) Membranes lacking PS II and NADP reductase","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Grana lamellae contain both PS I and PS II.\nB-(iv): Stroma lamellae lack PS II and the NADP reductase enzyme, so only cyclic flow can occur there.\nC-(i): The stroma contains the enzymes that synthesise sugar.\nD-(ii): The sugar formed is stored as starch granules within the chloroplast.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Melvin Calvin found that the first product of $CO_2$ fixation is a 3-carbon organic acid. He used radioactive $^{14}C$ to study photosynthesis in which organisms?","image":"","options":["Purple and green sulphur bacteria","Algae","Maize and sorghum leaves","Mint plants in a bell jar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Calvin worked just after World War II, when there were efforts to put radioisotopes to beneficial use.\nHe labelled $CO_2$ with $^{14}C$ and followed it in algal photosynthesis.\nThe sulphur bacteria were studied by van Niel, and mint in a bell jar was Priestley's experiment.\nMaize and sorghum are C4 plants. They were not part of Calvin's experiments.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Green plants are called autotrophs because they:","image":"","options":["obtain ready-made organic food by absorbing it from the soil","synthesise their own food by photosynthesis, using light energy","depend on other organisms for their organic compounds","release carbon dioxide during the day"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Green plants make the food they need by photosynthesis, so they are autotrophs.\nPhotosynthesis is a physico-chemical process in which light energy drives the synthesis of organic compounds.\nOption (1) is wrong because plants absorb water and minerals from the soil, not ready-made organic food.\nOption (3) describes heterotrophs, which depend on green plants for food.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"According to the chemiosmotic hypothesis, where do protons accumulate in a chloroplast during the light reaction to set up the gradient that drives ATP synthesis?","image":"","options":["In the stroma","In the thylakoid lumen","In the space between the outer and inner chloroplast membranes","In the cytoplasm surrounding the chloroplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In chloroplasts, ATP synthesis depends on a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane.\nProtons accumulate inside the thylakoid, in the lumen, so the stroma loses protons.\nIn mitochondria, protons accumulate in the intermembrane space. This makes option (3) tempting, but the space between the two chloroplast envelope membranes is not where the gradient forms.\nThe stroma is the side that loses protons, not the side that gains them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cyclic photophosphorylation produces ATP but not NADPH + H$^+$.\nReason (R): Stroma lamellae membranes contain PS II but lack PS I.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. In cyclic flow, the excited electron from PS I is not passed to NADP$^+$. It returns to PS I through the electron transport chain, so only ATP is made.\nR is false. Stroma lamellae have PS I but lack PS II and NADP reductase. This is why only cyclic flow can occur there.\nThe grana lamellae are the ones that have both photosystems.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $C_4$ plants form OAA as the first product but still use the Calvin cycle as their main biosynthetic pathway.\n(ii) The Calvin cycle operates only in $C_3$ plants.\n(iii) Carboxylation of one RuBP molecule yields two molecules of 3-PGA.\n(iv) When light is switched off, the biosynthetic phase stops at that very instant.\n(v) Six turns of the Calvin cycle are needed to form one glucose molecule.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in $C_4$ plants, the $CO_2$ released in the bundle sheath enters the Calvin cycle.\n(ii) Incorrect: the Calvin cycle occurs in all photosynthetic plants.\n(iii) Correct: RuBisCO converts RuBP + $CO_2$ into 2 × 3-PGA.\n(iv) Incorrect: the phase continues for some time in the dark, then stops.\n(v) Correct: 6 $CO_2$ and 6 turns give one glucose.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The water-splitting complex in the chloroplast is associated with:","image":"","options":["PS I, on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane","PS II, on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane","PS II, on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane","PS I, on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Splitting of water is linked to PS II. It replaces the electrons that PS II loses after excitation.\nNCERT states that PS II, and therefore the water-splitting complex, is located on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane.\nThis is why the protons released by water splitting collect in the lumen.\nThe options naming PS I are wrong because PS I gets its replacement electrons from PS II, not from water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The reaction centre of each photosystem is formed by","image":"","options":["Hundreds of chlorophyll a molecules bound to proteins","A single molecule of chlorophyll b","A single molecule of chlorophyll a","A carotenoid molecule bound to a cytochrome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In each photosystem, all pigments except one molecule of chlorophyll a form the light harvesting system, or antennae.\nThat one remaining chlorophyll a molecule forms the reaction centre.\nThe phrase 'hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins' describes the LHC, not the reaction centre.\nChlorophyll b and carotenoids are accessory pigments.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the site of photosynthesis is correct?","image":"","options":["Photosynthesis takes place only in leaves and in no other plant part","Photosynthesis takes place in green leaves and also in other green parts of the plant","Light reactions of photosynthesis occur in the stroma","Sugar synthesis occurs on the outer membrane of the chloroplast"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: photosynthesis is not restricted to leaves.\n(2) Correct: photosynthesis occurs mainly in green leaves but also in other green parts, such as young green stems.\n(3) Incorrect: light reactions occur on the thylakoid membranes, not in the stroma.\n(4) Incorrect: sugar synthesis occurs in the stroma.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which enzyme is absent from the mesophyll cells of a C4 plant like sorghum?","image":"","options":["PEP carboxylase","ATP synthase","RuBisCO","NADP reductase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT stresses that the mesophyll cells of C4 plants lack RuBisCO.\nPEPcase is present in the mesophyll cells and fixes $CO_2$ into OAA.\nRuBisCO is found in the bundle sheath cells, which in turn lack PEPcase.\nATP synthase and NADP reductase belong to the light reactions, which occur in mesophyll chloroplasts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Read the statements about the importance of photosynthesis.\nStatement I: Photosynthesis is the primary source of all food on earth.\nStatement II: Photosynthesis is responsible for the release of oxygen into the atmosphere by green plants.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives two reasons why photosynthesis is important.\nStatement I is correct: all food on earth ultimately comes from photosynthesis, because heterotrophs depend on green plants.\nStatement II is correct: green plants release oxygen into the atmosphere during photosynthesis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Melvin Calvin used radioactive $^{14}C$ to study photosynthesis in algae. What was the first $CO_2$ fixation product he identified?","image":"","options":["Oxaloacetic acid, a 4-carbon acid","3-phosphoglyceric acid, a 3-carbon organic acid","Ribulose bisphosphate, a 5-carbon sugar","Phosphoenol pyruvate, a 3-carbon compound"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Calvin traced labelled carbon from $^{14}CO_2$ in algae. He found that it first appeared in a 3-carbon organic acid, 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA).\nOAA is the first product in $C_4$ plants, not in Calvin's algal studies.\nRuBP is the acceptor molecule, not the product. PEP is the acceptor in $C_4$ mesophyll cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student repeats Ingenhousz's work. Two identical sealed bell jars each contain a mint plant and a candle that has just gone out. Jar X is kept in sunlight and jar Y in complete darkness for a few days. The candle in each jar is then rekindled without opening the jar. What is the most likely observation?","image":"","options":["The candle burns in both jars, because plants restore air in light and in dark alike","The candle burns in jar Y only, because plants purify air at night","The candle burns in jar X but goes out quickly in jar Y","The candle fails to burn in both jars, because plants cannot restore air"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ingenhousz used Priestley's set-up, placing it once in the dark and once in sunlight.\nHe showed that sunlight is essential for the plant process that purifies the air fouled by candles or animals.\nIn jar X, the plant photosynthesises and restores O2, so the candle burns. In jar Y, no restoring process occurs, so the candle soon goes out.\nOption (1) ignores this requirement for light.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which pigment is regarded as the chief pigment associated with photosynthesis in higher plants?","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll b","Carotenoids","Chlorophyll a","Xanthophylls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a absorbs light most strongly in the blue and red regions.\nThe rate of photosynthesis is also highest in these same regions.\nThis match shows that chlorophyll a is the chief photosynthetic pigment. It also forms the reaction centre of both photosystems.\nChlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids are accessory pigments. They pass absorbed energy on to chlorophyll a.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The correct overall equation of photosynthesis is written as 6CO$_2$ + 12H$_2$O → C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ + 6H$_2$O + 6O$_2$, rather than the simpler 6CO$_2$ + 6H$_2$O → C$_6$H$_{12}$O$_6$ + 6O$_2$. Which statement best explains why 12 molecules of water are shown as substrate?","image":"","options":["Six water molecules provide hydrogen for glucose, and the other six are needed only to keep the stomata open","All 12 oxygen atoms in the 6 O$_2$ must come from water, which needs 12 H$_2$O; the oxygen atoms of the 6 H$_2$O formed come from CO$_2$","Twelve water molecules are needed because both CO$_2$ and H$_2$O contribute oxygen to the O$_2$ released","The extra six water molecules are used only to regenerate RuBP in the Calvin cycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The O$_2$ released comes only from water. Six O$_2$ contain 12 oxygen atoms, so 12 H$_2$O must be split.\nThose 12 H$_2$O also supply 24 H atoms: 12 go into glucose and 12 form the 6 new H$_2$O.\nThe 12 oxygen atoms of 6 CO$_2$ end up in glucose (6 O) and in the 6 H$_2$O formed (6 O).\nThe simpler equation would wrongly suggest that some O$_2$ comes from CO$_2$, which is the error in option (3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Reduction of NADP$^+$ helps to increase the proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane.\nReason (R): NADP reductase is located on the stroma side of the membrane and uses protons from the stroma to reduce NADP$^+$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: reducing NADP$^+$ lowers the number of protons in the stroma, which widens the difference between stroma and lumen.\nR is true: NADP reductase sits on the stromal face of the membrane and takes up stromal protons to form NADPH + H$^+$.\nR explains A, because removing protons from the stroma is exactly how this step adds to the gradient.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the site of photosynthesis are correct?\n(i) The membrane system of the chloroplast traps light energy and synthesises ATP and NADPH.\n(ii) Enzymatic reactions in the stroma synthesise sugar, which in turn forms starch.\n(iii) The carbon reactions are called dark reactions because they take place only when light is absent.\n(iv) Photosynthesis occurs only in green leaves and in no other green part of the plant.\n(v) The chloroplast stroma contains ribosomes, starch granules and lipid droplets.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: grana and stroma lamellae trap light and form ATP and NADPH. These are the light reactions.\n(ii) Correct: sugar is made in the stroma by enzymes and is then stored as starch.\n(iii) Incorrect: 'dark' only means these reactions are not directly light driven. They depend on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, and they stop soon after light is withdrawn.\n(iv) Incorrect: other green parts also photosynthesise.\n(v) Correct: the chloroplast micrograph shows ribosomes, starch granules and lipid droplets in the stroma.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the light reaction is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The reaction centre of PS I is a chlorophyll a molecule with an absorption peak at 700 nm","In each photosystem, all pigments except one chlorophyll a molecule form the light harvesting antenna","The water-splitting complex is associated with PS II, located on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane","PS I and PS II are named in the sequence in which they function during the light reaction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the PS I reaction centre is P700.\n(2) Correct: the antenna is made of all pigments except the single reaction-centre chlorophyll a.\n(3) Correct: water splitting is linked to PS II on the lumen side of the membrane.\n(4) Incorrect: the photosystems are named in the order of their discovery. In fact, PS II functions first.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Jan Ingenhousz worked with an aquatic plant. Which observation led him to conclude that only the green parts of a plant release oxygen?","image":"","options":["A candle kept beside the plant in a closed bell jar went out faster in sunlight","Starch was found only in the green parts after iodine testing","Small bubbles formed around the green parts in bright sunlight but not in the dark","Aerobic bacteria gathered around the roots in red light"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ingenhousz saw small bubbles form around the green parts of an aquatic plant in bright sunlight. No bubbles formed in the dark.\nHe later identified these bubbles as oxygen, so he concluded that only green parts release O$_2$, and only in light.\nThe starch test in option (2) relates to Sachs and to the variegated-leaf experiment, not to Ingenhousz.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with respect to the Hatch and Slack pathway:\nList-I\n(a) PEP carboxylase\n(b) RuBisCO\n(c) Malic acid or aspartic acid\n(d) 3-carbon molecule released after decarboxylation\nList-II\n(i) Returned to the mesophyll and converted to PEP\n(ii) Fixes $CO_2$ in the mesophyll forming OAA\n(iii) Present in bundle sheath cells, absent in mesophyll cells\n(iv) Transported from mesophyll to bundle sheath cells","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) PEPcase fixes $CO_2$ onto PEP in the mesophyll, forming OAA. This matches (ii).\n(b) RuBisCO is found in the bundle sheath and absent from the mesophyll. This matches (iii).\n(c) Malic or aspartic acid forms from OAA in the mesophyll and moves to the bundle sheath. This matches (iv).\n(d) The 3-carbon molecule left after decarboxylation goes back to the mesophyll to regenerate PEP. This matches (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assume that non-cyclic photophosphorylation alone supplies ATP and NADPH in equal numbers, and that the chloroplast receives exactly enough NADPH to make one glucose molecule by the Calvin cycle. How many additional ATP molecules must then come from cyclic photophosphorylation?","image":"","options":["0","12","18","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: per $CO_2$ fixed, the cycle uses 3 ATP and 2 NADPH, and one glucose needs 6 turns.\nTotal requirement: 6 × 3 = 18 ATP and 6 × 2 = 12 NADPH.\nNon-cyclic flow provides 12 NADPH and, by the stated assumption, only 12 ATP.\nShortfall = 18 − 12 = 6 ATP, which cyclic photophosphorylation supplies, since it makes only ATP.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (components involved in chemiosmosis in the chloroplast) with List-II (their location and role).\nList-I:\n(a) $CF_0$\n(b) $CF_1$\n(c) Water-splitting complex\n(d) NADP reductase\nList-II:\n(i) Faces the stroma; removes protons from the stroma while reducing $NADP^+$\n(ii) Embedded in the thylakoid membrane; forms a channel for facilitated diffusion of protons\n(iii) Protrudes into the stroma; undergoes a conformational change that leads to ATP synthesis\n(iv) On the inner side of the membrane; releases protons into the lumen","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) $CF_0$ is the transmembrane part of ATP synthase. It allows protons to diffuse from the lumen to the stroma, so it matches (ii).\n(b) $CF_1$ sticks out on the stroma face. The energy from proton flow changes its shape, and this drives ATP synthesis, so it matches (iii).\n(c) The water-splitting complex works on the inner side and adds protons to the lumen, so it matches (iv).\n(d) NADP reductase is on the stroma side and uses up stromal protons, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: C$_3$ plants reach CO$_2$ saturation only beyond about 450 µL L$^{-1}$, so present atmospheric CO$_2$ levels limit their photosynthesis.\nStatement II: Under low light intensity, C$_4$ plants respond to high CO$_2$ concentration much more strongly than C$_3$ plants do.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. C$_4$ plants saturate at about 360 µL L$^{-1}$, while C$_3$ plants keep responding up to beyond 450 µL L$^{-1}$. This is why CO$_2$-enriched greenhouses raise yields of tomatoes and bell pepper.\nStatement II is incorrect. At low light, neither C$_3$ nor C$_4$ plants respond to high CO$_2$. Both respond only at high light intensities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about factors affecting photosynthesis is correct?","image":"","options":["At low light intensity, both C$_3$ and C$_4$ plants show a marked rise in photosynthesis when CO$_2$ concentration is increased","C$_4$ plants saturate at about 360 $\\mu L\\,L^{-1}$ CO$_2$ whereas C$_3$ plants saturate only beyond 450 $\\mu L\\,L^{-1}$, so present atmospheric CO$_2$ limits C$_3$ plants","Light reactions are affected by temperature much more strongly than the enzymatic dark reactions","Water affects photosynthesis mainly because it is a direct reactant in the light reaction, and not through its effects on the plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: at low light, neither C$_3$ nor C$_4$ plants respond to high CO$_2$.\n(2) Correct: C$_4$ plants saturate near 360 $\\mu L\\,L^{-1}$, while C$_3$ plants keep responding up to beyond 450 $\\mu L\\,L^{-1}$. Present CO$_2$ levels therefore limit C$_3$ plants.\n(3) Incorrect: the dark reactions are enzymatic and strongly temperature controlled. Light reactions are affected much less.\n(4) Incorrect: water acts mainly through its effect on the plant. Water stress closes stomata, which cuts CO$_2$ supply, and causes wilting.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Isolated chloroplasts are illuminated only with light of wavelength 700 nm, and no light of 680 nm or shorter is supplied. Which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["NADPH is formed but ATP synthesis stops, because PS II is inactive","O$_2$ continues to be evolved, because PS I can split water","ATP is formed by cyclic electron flow, but no NADPH is formed and no O$_2$ is evolved","Both ATP and NADPH are formed by non-cyclic photophosphorylation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"P680 of PS II is not excited by 700 nm light, but P700 of PS I is.\nNCERT states that cyclic photophosphorylation occurs when only wavelengths beyond 680 nm are available.\nIn cyclic flow, the electron returns to PS I, so ATP is made but NADP$^+$ is not reduced.\nWater splitting is linked to PS II, so with PS II inactive no O$_2$ is released.\nNon-cyclic flow needs both photosystems working in series, so option (4) fails.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A variegated leaf (with green and white patches) was kept in light and then tested with iodine for starch. Which observation is expected?","image":"","options":["The whole leaf turns blue-black","Only the white patches turn blue-black","Only the green patches turn blue-black","No part of the leaf turns blue-black"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A blue-black colour with iodine shows that starch is present, which means photosynthesis took place there.\nThe white patches of a variegated leaf have no chlorophyll, so they cannot photosynthesise.\nOnly the green patches form starch.\nThis shows that chlorophyll is needed for photosynthesis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are direct products of the splitting of water during the light reaction?\nH$^+$ ions, O$_2$, electrons, NADPH, ATP, CO$_2$","image":"","options":["2","4","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The reaction is $2H_2O \\rightarrow 4H^+ + O_2 + 4e^-$.\nH$^+$: Yes, released into the lumen.\nO$_2$: Yes, one of the net products of photosynthesis.\nElectrons: Yes, they replace those lost by PS II.\nNADPH: No, it is formed later when PS I electrons reduce NADP$^+$.\nATP: No, it is made by ATP synthase using the proton gradient.\nCO$_2$: No, it is a raw material, not a product of water splitting.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the light reaction, the water-splitting complex is associated with which photosystem, and on which side of the thylakoid membrane is it located?","image":"","options":["PS I, on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane","PS II, on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane","PS II, on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane","PS I, on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that water splitting is associated with PS II.\nPS II is physically located on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane.\nSo the protons and O2 from water splitting are released into the lumen. This helps build the proton gradient.\nThe stroma-side options fail because NADP reductase sits on the stroma side, not the water-splitting complex.\nThe PS I options fail because PS I does not split water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cyclic photophosphorylation results in the synthesis of ATP but not of NADPH + H$^+$.\nReason (R): The stroma lamellae membranes, a possible site of cyclic flow, lack PS II as well as the NADP reductase enzyme.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In cyclic flow, the excited electron from PS I is cycled back to the PS I complex through the electron transport chain. So only ATP is made.\nR is true. NCERT notes that stroma lamellae lack both PS II and NADP reductase.\nWithout NADP reductase, electrons cannot be passed on to NADP$^+$. So R correctly explains why NADPH is not formed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The enzyme that is not found in a C$_3$ plant is","image":"","options":["RuBP Carboxylase","PEP Carboxylase","NADP reductase","ATP synthase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"RuBisCO (RuBP carboxylase) fixes CO$_2$ in the Calvin cycle of C$_3$ plants.\nNADP reductase and ATP synthase work in the light reaction of all green plants.\nAccording to NCERT, PEP carboxylase is the enzyme for primary CO$_2$ fixation in C$_4$ (and CAM) plants. It plays no part in the C$_3$ pathway, so it is the intended answer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist, around 1854, provided evidence that glucose is produced when plants grow, that it is usually stored as starch, and that the green substance of plants lies in special bodies within cells?","image":"","options":["Jan Ingenhousz","Joseph Priestley","Julius von Sachs","T.W. Engelmann"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Julius von Sachs (about 1854) showed that glucose is produced when plants grow and is usually stored as starch.\nHis later studies located chlorophyll in special bodies, later called chloroplasts, and showed that glucose is made in the green parts.\nIngenhousz worked on sunlight and O2 bubbles, not on glucose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is NOT a part of the light reaction (photochemical phase) of photosynthesis?","image":"","options":["Splitting of water","Release of oxygen","Carboxylation of ribulose bisphosphate","Formation of ATP and NADPH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The light reaction includes light absorption, water splitting, oxygen release and the formation of ATP and NADPH.\nThese steps take place on the thylakoid membranes.\nCarboxylation of RuBP by RuBisCO is the first step of the Calvin cycle. It is a biosynthetic (carbon) reaction in the stroma.\nIt uses the ATP and NADPH made by the light reaction but is not part of it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many molecules of ATP and NADPH are consumed by the Calvin cycle to produce two molecules of glucose?","image":"","options":["36 ATP and 24 NADPH","18 ATP and 12 NADPH","24 ATP and 36 NADPH","36 ATP and 36 NADPH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each CO$_2$ fixed needs 3 ATP: 2 for reduction and 1 for regenerating RuBP. It also needs 2 NADPH.\nOne glucose needs 6 CO$_2$, which costs 18 ATP and 12 NADPH.\nTwo glucose molecules need 12 CO$_2$, which costs 36 ATP and 24 NADPH.\nOption (2) is the cost for only one glucose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Photorespiration does not occur in $C_4$ plants.\nReason (R): In $C_4$ plants, C4 acids formed in the mesophyll are broken down in the bundle sheath cells to release $CO_2$. This raises the $CO_2$ concentration at the RuBisCO site.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that $C_4$ plants lack photorespiration.\nR is true: decarboxylation of malic or aspartic acid in the bundle sheath cells releases $CO_2$ and raises its intracellular concentration.\nBinding of $CO_2$ and $O_2$ to RuBisCO is competitive. A high $CO_2$ level makes RuBisCO act as a carboxylase and minimises its oxygenase activity, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When light is switched off, the biosynthetic phase of photosynthesis continues for some time and then stops.\nStatement II: The biosynthetic phase is driven directly by light energy absorbed by chlorophyll.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Sugar synthesis carries on briefly after the light goes off, then stops. It starts again when light returns.\nStatement II is incorrect. The biosynthetic phase does not depend directly on light. It depends on the light-reaction products, ATP and NADPH.\nThat is why it continues only until the existing stock of ATP and NADPH is used up.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) van Niel based his conclusions on studies of purple and green bacteria.\n(ii) In van Niel's general equation $2H_2A + CO_2 \\rightarrow 2A + CH_2O + H_2O$, for green plants $H_2A$ is water and the oxidation product corresponds to O2.\n(iii) van Niel concluded that the O2 evolved by green plants comes from CO2.\n(iv) The overall photosynthesis equation represents a single chemical reaction.\n(v) In Engelmann's experiment, bacteria accumulated mainly in the blue and red regions of the spectrum.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: van Niel studied purple and green bacteria.\n(ii) Correct: in green plants, H2O is the hydrogen donor and is oxidised to O2.\n(iii) Incorrect: he inferred that O2 comes from H2O, not CO2.\n(iv) Incorrect: the equation summarises a multistep process.\n(v) Correct: the bacteria gathered in blue and red light.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Priestley found that a mouse soon suffocated in a closed bell jar. When a mint plant was placed in the same bell jar, the mouse stayed alive. What hypothesis did Priestley form from this?","image":"","options":["Plants release glucose into the air that animals use as food","Plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove","Plants absorb all gases released by animals and make the air empty","Plants purify the air only when kept in sunlight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Priestley saw that a burning candle or a breathing animal somehow 'damages' the air in a closed space.\nA mint plant kept in the same jar allowed the mouse to live and the candle to keep burning.\nHe therefore hypothesised that plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove.\nOption (4) is tempting, but it was Ingenhousz, not Priestley, who later showed that sunlight is essential for this.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In C3 plants, when RuBisCO binds $O_2$ instead of $CO_2$, RuBP is converted into which of the following?","image":"","options":["Two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate","One molecule of phosphoglycerate and one molecule of phosphoglycolate","One molecule of oxaloacetic acid","One molecule of phosphoenol pyruvate and one molecule of $CO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: RuBisCO's active site can bind both $CO_2$ and $O_2$. The two gases compete for it.\nWhen $O_2$ binds, RuBP forms one phosphoglycerate (3C) and one phosphoglycolate (2C). This pathway is called photorespiration.\nOption (1) is the normal carboxylation product, which forms only when $CO_2$ binds.\nOAA (option 3) is the first product of PEP carboxylase in C4 plants.\nPEP (option 4) is the C4 acceptor molecule, not an oxygenation product.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Primary $CO_2$ acceptor in $C_3$ plants\n(b) Primary $CO_2$ acceptor in $C_4$ plants\n(c) First stable $CO_2$ fixation product in $C_4$ plants\n(d) First $CO_2$ fixation product in $C_3$ plants\nList-II\n(i) Oxaloacetic acid\n(ii) 3-phosphoglyceric acid\n(iii) Ribulose bisphosphate\n(iv) Phosphoenol pyruvate","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $C_3$ plants accept $CO_2$ on RuBP, a 5-carbon ketose sugar, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) $C_4$ mesophyll cells use PEP (3C) as the acceptor, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) The first stable $C_4$ product is OAA (4C), so (c)-(i).\n(d) The first $C_3$ product is 3-PGA (3C), so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the concentration of $CO_2$ in the atmosphere lies in the range:","image":"","options":["0.3 to 0.4 per cent","3 to 4 per cent","0.003 to 0.004 per cent","0.03 to 0.04 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Atmospheric CO2 is very low, between 0.03 and 0.04 per cent.\nThis low level is why CO2 is called the major limiting factor for photosynthesis.\nThe value 0.3 to 0.4 per cent is ten times too high, a common slip with decimals.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which range of wavelength (in nm) is called photosynthetically active radiation (PAR)?","image":"","options":["100 - 390","390 - 430","400 - 700","760 – 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Photosynthetically active radiation (PAR) is the visible light plants use for photosynthesis, about 400 to 700 nm.\nThe 100-390 nm range is ultraviolet, and 760-10,000 nm is infrared. Neither drives photosynthesis.\nThe 390-430 nm range covers only a small violet band, not the whole PAR range.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A strip of black paper is fixed across the middle of a variegated leaf, covering both green and white patches. The plant is kept in bright light for several hours, and the leaf is then tested with iodine. Which region(s) will show starch?","image":"","options":["Green patches under the black paper as well as green patches outside it","Only the green patches outside the black paper","All uncovered patches, both green and white","Only the white patches outside the black paper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Starch forms only where photosynthesis occurs, and that needs both chlorophyll and light.\nGreen patches under the paper have chlorophyll but no light, so they form no starch.\nWhite uncovered patches get light but have no chlorophyll, so they form no starch.\nOnly green patches outside the paper have both factors and form starch.\nOption (3) is tempting, but it ignores the need for chlorophyll.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pigment) with List-II (colour of its band on a paper chromatogram).\nList-I:\nA. Chlorophyll a\nB. Chlorophyll b\nC. Xanthophylls\nD. Carotenoids\nList-II:\nI. Yellow\nII. Yellow green\nIII. Bright or blue green\nIV. Yellow to yellow-orange","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Chlorophyll a forms a bright or blue-green band.\nB-II: Chlorophyll b forms a yellow-green band.\nC-I: Xanthophylls form a yellow band.\nD-IV: Carotenoids form a yellow to yellow-orange band.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A $C_3$ plant synthesises 3 molecules of glucose through the Calvin cycle. What are the number of turns of the cycle, the total ATP used and the total NADPH used, respectively?","image":"","options":["6, 18, 12","18, 36, 54","18, 54, 36","3, 9, 6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each turn fixes one $CO_2$. One glucose ($C_6$) needs 6 turns, so 3 glucose need 18 turns.\nEach $CO_2$ fixed uses 3 ATP: 2 in reduction and 1 in regeneration of RuBP. So 18 × 3 = 54 ATP.\nEach $CO_2$ fixed uses 2 NADPH, all in reduction. So 18 × 2 = 36 NADPH.\nOption (1) gives the values for only one glucose. Option (2) swaps the ATP and NADPH values.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"On a hot, dry, bright afternoon, a wheat ($C_3$) plant partially closes its stomata. As a result, the $CO_2$ to $O_2$ ratio inside the leaf falls. Which outcome is most consistent with NCERT?","image":"","options":["RuBisCO switches entirely to PEP carboxylase activity, so OAA becomes the first product","More RuBP is oxygenated, forming phosphoglycolate; $CO_2$ is released and ATP is used, so net $CO_2$ fixation falls","Photorespiration increases ATP and NADPH production, which compensates for the lower carboxylation","RuBisCO's affinity for $O_2$ drops, so carboxylation rises as $CO_2$ falls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Water stress closes stomata and lowers $CO_2$ availability inside the leaf.\nStep 2: Binding of $CO_2$ and $O_2$ to RuBisCO is competitive and depends on their relative concentrations. Less $CO_2$ means more oxygenation.\nStep 3: Oxygenation forms phosphoglycolate, releases $CO_2$ and consumes ATP, so net fixation falls.\nOption (1) fails because RuBisCO is a different enzyme from PEPcase. Option (3) fails because photorespiration makes no ATP or NADPH.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Calling the biosynthetic phase of photosynthesis the 'dark reaction' is a misnomer.\nReason (R): The biosynthetic phase is not directly light driven but depends on ATP and NADPH from the light reaction, so it continues only briefly after light is withdrawn and then stops.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The so-called dark reactions do not take place in darkness, and they are not light-independent.\nR is true. These reactions use ATP and NADPH produced by the light reactions.\nWhen light is removed, synthesis continues briefly and then stops. It restarts when light is given again.\nThis dependence on light-made products is exactly why the name 'dark' is misleading, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"During photorespiration in a $C_3$ plant, RuBisCO combines RuBP with $O_2$. What are the products of this reaction?","image":"","options":["Two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate","One molecule of phosphoglycerate and one molecule of phosphoglycolate","One molecule of oxaloacetic acid and one molecule of pyruvate","Two molecules of phosphoglycolate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When RuBisCO acts as an oxygenase, RuBP (5C) binds $O_2$ instead of $CO_2$.\nThis gives one phosphoglycerate (3C) and one phosphoglycolate (2C).\nTwo molecules of 3-PGA form only when RuBP is carboxylated, so option (1) is the tempting wrong choice.\nOAA is the first product of the $C_4$ pathway, and RuBisCO does not form it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (experimental set-up) with List-II (what it shows or what role it plays):\nList-I\n(a) Variegated leaf kept in light\n(b) Leaf partially covered with black paper, kept in light\n(c) Leaf part enclosed in a tube with KOH-soaked cotton\n(d) Leaf part left exposed to air in the KOH set-up\nList-II\n(i) Light is required\n(ii) Serves as the control\n(iii) Chlorophyll is required\n(iv) CO2 is required","image":"","options":["a-(iii), b-(i), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(i), b-(iii), c-(iv), d-(ii)","a-(iii), b-(i), c-(ii), d-(iv)","a-(iv), b-(i), c-(iii), d-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Variegated leaf: only the green parts form starch, so chlorophyll is required (iii).\n(b) Black-paper cover: the covered part forms no starch, so light is required (i).\n(c) KOH tube: CO2 is removed and no starch forms, so CO2 is required (iv).\n(d) The exposed part has all factors present and forms starch, so it is the control for comparison (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Chlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids are called accessory pigments mainly because they:","image":"","options":["absorb light and pass the absorbed energy on to chlorophyll a","act as the reaction centre molecules of both photosystems","absorb light only in the far-red region beyond 700 nm","fix CO$_2$ directly into carbohydrates in the stroma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a is the major pigment that traps light.\nThe other thylakoid pigments also absorb light, but they hand the energy on to chlorophyll a. That supporting role is why they are called accessory pigments.\nThe reaction centre is a single chlorophyll a molecule, so option (2) is wrong.\nCO$_2$ fixation is an enzymatic reaction in the stroma, not a pigment function, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about chemiosmosis in the chloroplast are correct?\n(i) Protons accumulate inside the thylakoid lumen.\n(ii) The CF$_1$ part of ATP synthase is embedded in the thylakoid membrane and forms the proton channel.\n(iii) NADP reductase is located on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane.\n(iv) The primary electron acceptor that picks up a proton is located towards the inner (lumen) side of the membrane.\n(v) A measurable decrease in pH occurs in the lumen.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: protons come from water splitting and the H-carrier, so they build up in the lumen.\n(ii) Incorrect: CF$_0$ is the embedded channel. CF$_1$ protrudes into the stroma.\n(iii) Correct: NADP reductase is on the stroma side and removes stromal protons.\n(iv) Incorrect: the primary acceptor lies towards the outer (stroma) side. It takes up a proton from the stroma.\n(v) Correct: the lumen pH falls.\nThat gives 3 correct statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the light-harvesting machinery of the thylakoid:\n(i) In each photosystem, all the pigments except one molecule of chlorophyll a form the antenna.\n(ii) The reaction centre chlorophyll a is identical in PS I and PS II, with an absorption peak at 700 nm.\n(iii) PS I and PS II are numbered according to the order in which they act during the light reaction.\n(iv) The light harvesting complexes consist of hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins.\n(v) The reaction centre of PS II has its absorption maximum at 680 nm.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in each photosystem, every pigment except one chlorophyll a molecule makes up the light-harvesting antenna.\n(ii) Incorrect: the reaction centres differ. PS I has P700 and PS II has P680.\n(iii) Incorrect: the photosystems are named in the order of their discovery. In the Z scheme, PS II acts before PS I.\n(iv) Correct: the LHCs contain hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins.\n(v) Correct: the PS II reaction centre, P680, absorbs maximally at 680 nm.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Engelmann's experiment, aerobic bacteria were used as indicators of the sites of O$_2$ evolution along the alga.\nStatement II: The action spectrum Engelmann obtained matched the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a alone exactly, wavelength for wavelength.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Aerobic bacteria move to O$_2$-rich regions, so where they collected showed where the alga was evolving O$_2$.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says the action spectrum only roughly resembles the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b.\nThere is no complete one-to-one overlap with chlorophyll a, because accessory pigments also absorb light and pass the energy to chlorophyll a.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Photosystem I and Photosystem II are numbered according to the","image":"","options":["Sequence in which they function during the light reaction","Sequence of their discovery","Wavelength of light absorbed by their reaction centres","Number of pigment molecules in their antennae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that PS I and PS II are named in the sequence of their discovery.\nThey are not named in the sequence in which they function.\nIn the Z scheme, PS II acts first and PS I acts second. So naming by order of function is the tempting but wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Ingenhousz observed that an aquatic plant formed small bubbles around its green parts in bright sunlight but not in the dark.\nStatement II: Ingenhousz identified these bubbles as carbon dioxide released by the plant.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In bright sunlight, bubbles formed around the green parts of the aquatic plant, and none formed in the dark.\nStatement II is incorrect. Ingenhousz identified the bubbles as oxygen, not CO2.\nThis showed that only the green parts of plants release oxygen, and only in light.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II with reference to the thylakoid membrane and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. $CF_0$\nB. $CF_1$\nC. NADP reductase\nD. Water-splitting complex\nList-II\n(i) Removes protons from the stroma while reducing $NADP^+$\n(ii) Releases protons into the lumen, on the inner side of the membrane\n(iii) Transmembrane channel allowing facilitated diffusion of protons\n(iv) Undergoes a conformational change that leads to ATP synthesis","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(iii): $CF_0$ is embedded in the membrane and forms the proton channel.\nB-(iv): $CF_1$ protrudes into the stroma. Proton flow causes its conformational change, and this drives ATP synthesis.\nC-(i): NADP reductase sits on the stroma side and uses stromal protons to reduce $NADP^+$.\nD-(ii): the water-splitting complex is linked to PS II on the inner side, so its protons enter the lumen.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A leaf fixes enough $CO_2$ through the Calvin cycle to make 3 molecules of glucose. How many ATP and NADPH molecules are consumed in total?","image":"","options":["36 ATP and 36 NADPH","54 ATP and 54 NADPH","18 ATP and 12 NADPH","54 ATP and 36 NADPH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each $CO_2$ needs 3 ATP: 2 for reduction and 1 for regeneration. It also needs 2 NADPH.\nOne glucose needs 6 $CO_2$, which is 6 turns of the cycle. That uses 18 ATP and 12 NADPH.\nFor 3 glucose: 3 × 18 = 54 ATP and 3 × 12 = 36 NADPH.\nOption (1) leaves out the ATP used in regeneration. Option (3) is the cost of only one glucose.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Purple and green sulphur bacteria carry out photosynthesis without releasing O2.\nReason (R): In these bacteria, H2S is the hydrogen donor, and its oxidation product is sulphur or sulphate.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: van Niel noted that some organisms do not release O2 during photosynthesis.\nR is true: in purple and green sulphur bacteria the hydrogen donor is H2S, which is oxidised to sulphur or sulphate depending on the organism.\nSince water is not the hydrogen donor, no O2 is formed. R therefore correctly explains A.\nThis comparison led van Niel to infer that the O2 released by green plants comes from H2O.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the photorespiratory pathway is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It results in the release of $CO_2$","It consumes ATP","It produces NADPH, which compensates for the loss of fixed carbon","Its biological function is not yet known"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: photorespiration releases $CO_2$.\n(2) Correct: it uses ATP.\n(3) Incorrect: no sugar, ATP or NADPH is made in this pathway, so nothing compensates for the carbon lost.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states that its biological function is not known.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A leaf extract is separated by paper chromatography. Match the pigment in List-I with the colour of its band in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Chlorophyll a\nB. Chlorophyll b\nC. Xanthophylls\nD. Carotenoids\nList-II\n(i) Yellow\n(ii) Yellow-green\n(iii) Yellow to yellow-orange\n(iv) Bright or blue-green","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Chlorophyll a gives a bright or blue-green band, so A-(iv).\nB. Chlorophyll b gives a yellow-green band, so B-(ii).\nC. Xanthophylls give a yellow band, so C-(i).\nD. Carotenoids give a yellow to yellow-orange band, so D-(iii).\nOption (2) swaps the two chlorophylls. Option (3) swaps xanthophylls and carotenoids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The light reaction produces ATP, NADPH and $O_2$. Which of these is NOT used to drive sugar synthesis in the stroma and instead diffuses out of the chloroplast?","image":"","options":["ATP","NADPH","$O_2$","Both ATP and NADPH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The products of the light reaction are ATP, NADPH and $O_2$.\nATP and NADPH power the biosynthetic phase, which fixes $CO_2$ and makes sugars.\n$O_2$ is not used in this phase; it diffuses out of the chloroplast.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of the Hatch and Slack pathway in the correct order:\nP. Breakdown of the C4 acid in bundle sheath cells releasing $CO_2$\nQ. Fixation of $CO_2$ by PEPcase forming OAA\nR. Conversion of OAA to malic acid or aspartic acid\nS. Entry of released $CO_2$ into the Calvin cycle through RuBisCO\nU. Transport of the 4-carbon acid from mesophyll to bundle sheath cells","image":"","options":["Q → U → R → P → S","Q → R → U → P → S","R → Q → U → P → S","Q → R → P → U → S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Q comes first. PEPcase fixes $CO_2$ onto PEP in the mesophyll, forming OAA.\nR follows. Still within the mesophyll, OAA forms malic or aspartic acid.\nU comes next. These 4-carbon acids move to the bundle sheath cells.\nP happens there. The acids are broken down to release $CO_2$ and a 3-carbon molecule.\nS is last. The released $CO_2$ enters the Calvin cycle through RuBisCO.\nOption (1) wrongly sends OAA to the bundle sheath before it is converted. Option (4) places decarboxylation before transport.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In T.W. Engelmann's experiment, a filament of the green alga Cladophora was lit with light split by a prism and kept in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. The bacteria gathered mainly in the blue and red regions. What did this experiment first describe?","image":"","options":["The absorption spectrum of chlorophyll b","The first action spectrum of photosynthesis","The site of starch storage in chloroplasts","The release of CO$_2$ during photorespiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aerobic bacteria move towards oxygen, so they marked the sites where Cladophora evolved O$_2$.\nThey crowded in blue and red light, so photosynthesis was highest at those wavelengths.\nA plot of photosynthetic rate against wavelength is an action spectrum. This was the first one described.\nIt only roughly resembles the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b. It is not itself an absorption spectrum, so option (1) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are accessory pigments of photosynthesis?\nChlorophyll a, chlorophyll b, xanthophylls, carotenoids, P680 reaction-centre pigment","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a is not an accessory pigment. It is the chief pigment.\nChlorophyll b is an accessory pigment.\nXanthophylls are accessory pigments.\nCarotenoids are accessory pigments.\nP680 is not an accessory pigment. It is the reaction-centre chlorophyll a of PS II.\nThat gives 3 accessory pigments.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"PEP is primary CO$_2$ acceptor in","image":"","options":["C$_4$ plants","C$_3$ plants","C$_2$ plants","Both C$_3$ and C$_4$ plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In C$_4$ plants, phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) in the mesophyll cells is the first molecule to accept CO$_2$. PEP carboxylase catalyses this step and forms oxaloacetic acid, a 4-carbon compound.\nC$_3$ plants use RuBP as their primary CO$_2$ acceptor, via RuBisCO. So 'both C$_3$ and C$_4$' is wrong.\nThe C$_2$ pathway is photorespiration, which does not fix CO$_2$ using PEP.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule in the C4 pathway) with List-II (its role).\nList-I\n(a) Phosphoenol pyruvate\n(b) Oxaloacetic acid\n(c) Malic acid / aspartic acid\n(d) 3-carbon molecule formed in bundle sheath\nList-II\n(i) Carried from mesophyll to bundle sheath cells\n(ii) Primary $CO_2$ acceptor in mesophyll\n(iii) First C4 acid formed on $CO_2$ fixation\n(iv) Returned to mesophyll to regenerate the acceptor","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): PEP (3C) accepts $CO_2$ in the mesophyll.\n(b)-(iii): OAA is the first 4-carbon acid formed.\n(c)-(i): OAA is converted to malic or aspartic acid in the mesophyll. These acids are then carried to the bundle sheath cells.\n(d)-(iv): In the bundle sheath, decarboxylation releases $CO_2$ and a 3C molecule. The 3C molecule returns to the mesophyll and is converted back to PEP.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cyclic photophosphorylation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Only Photosystem I is functional and the excited electron is cycled back to it through the electron transport chain","It possibly occurs in stroma lamellae, which lack PS II and NADP reductase","It occurs when only light of wavelengths beyond 680 nm is available","The cyclic flow of electrons results in the synthesis of both ATP and NADPH + $H^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in cyclic flow the electron from PS I returns to PS I.\n(2) Correct: stroma lamellae lack both PS II and NADP reductase.\n(3) Correct: light beyond 680 nm cannot excite P680, so only PS I operates.\n(4) Incorrect: the electron never reaches $NADP^+$. Cyclic flow therefore makes only ATP and no NADPH.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) P680\n(b) P700\n(c) Cytochromes\n(d) NADP$^+$\nList-II\n(i) Final electron acceptor in non-cyclic flow\n(ii) Reaction centre of PS I\n(iii) Electron transport system between PS II and PS I\n(iv) Reaction centre of PS II","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) P680 absorbs at 680 nm and is the reaction centre of PS II, so it matches (iv).\n(b) P700 absorbs at 700 nm and is the reaction centre of PS I, so it matches (ii).\n(c) Cytochromes form the electron transport system that carries electrons downhill from PS II to PS I, so they match (iii).\n(d) NADP$^+$ is reduced to NADPH + H$^+$ at the end of the Z scheme, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are internal (plant) factors affecting photosynthesis?\n(i) Number and age of leaves\n(ii) Size of chloroplasts\n(iii) Amount of chlorophyll\n(iv) Internal CO$_2$ concentration\n(v) Availability of sunlight\n(vi) Temperature\n(vii) Availability of water","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Internal factors depend on the plant's genetic predisposition and growth.\n(i), (ii), (iii) Internal: leaf number, size, age and orientation, chloroplasts and chlorophyll amount are plant factors.\n(iv) Internal: NCERT lists internal CO$_2$ concentration as a plant factor. Atmospheric CO$_2$ concentration, by contrast, is external.\n(v), (vi), (vii) External: sunlight, temperature and water are external factors.\nSo there are four internal factors.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Calvin cycle, which molecule accepts $CO_2$ and, after fixation, gives rise to the 3-carbon product?","image":"","options":["Phosphoenol pyruvate, a 3-carbon compound","A 2-carbon compound that combines with $CO_2$","Ribulose bisphosphate, a 5-carbon ketose sugar","3-phosphoglyceric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The primary $CO_2$ acceptor of the Calvin cycle is ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP), a 5-carbon ketose sugar.\nIts carboxylation yields two molecules of 3-PGA.\nScientists first expected a 2-carbon acceptor, but no such compound exists in this pathway.\nPEP is the acceptor in $C_4$ mesophyll cells, and PGA is the product, not the acceptor.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In T.W. Engelmann's experiment, light split by a prism fell on the green alga Cladophora kept in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. The bacteria crowded mainly in two regions of the spectrum. What does this observation directly indicate?","image":"","options":["Oxygen evolution, and hence photosynthesis, was highest in the blue and red regions of light","The bacteria were photosynthesising most actively in the blue and red regions","Carbon dioxide release by the alga was highest in the green region of light","Chlorophyll absorbs green light most strongly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Aerobic bacteria need oxygen, so they gather wherever the alga gives off the most O$_2$.\nThey crowded in the blue and red regions. This means photosynthesis, measured as O$_2$ evolution, was highest there.\nThis gave the first action spectrum, which roughly matches the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b.\nThe bacteria only acted as O$_2$ detectors; they did not photosynthesise themselves.\nGreen light is mostly reflected by chlorophyll, not absorbed strongly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"As the proton gradient builds up during the light reaction, what happens to the pH of the thylakoid lumen?","image":"","options":["It decreases","It increases","It stays exactly neutral","It first rises and then becomes equal to the stromal pH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Protons accumulate in the thylakoid lumen.\nA higher concentration of H$^+$ means a lower pH.\nNCERT notes a measurable decrease in pH in the lumen.\nAt the same time, the stroma loses protons, so its pH does not fall.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Joseph Priestley\n(b) Jan Ingenhousz\n(c) T.W. Engelmann\n(d) Cornelius van Niel\nList-II\n(i) First action spectrum of photosynthesis\n(ii) O2 evolved by green plants comes from water\n(iii) Plants restore air damaged by a candle or mouse\n(iv) Sunlight is essential for the plant to purify air","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Priestley used a bell jar with a candle, mouse and mint and concluded that plants restore the air, so (iii).\n(b) Ingenhousz ran the same set-up in light and in dark and showed sunlight is essential, so (iv).\n(c) Engelmann used a prism, Cladophora and aerobic bacteria to describe the first action spectrum, so (i).\n(d) van Niel inferred from bacteria that O2 comes from H2O, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Because the first stable product of the Calvin cycle (PGA) has 3 carbons, scientists long searched for a 2-carbon CO$_2$ acceptor. The acceptor actually turned out to be:","image":"","options":["Phosphoenol pyruvate, a 3-carbon compound","Oxaloacetic acid, a 4-carbon acid","Ribulose bisphosphate, a 5-carbon ketose sugar","Phosphoglycolate, a 2-carbon compound"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The primary CO$_2$ acceptor in the Calvin cycle is RuBP, a 5-carbon ketose sugar.\nRuBP (5C) plus CO$_2$ (1C) gives 6 carbons, which appear as two molecules of 3-PGA.\nPEP is the acceptor in C$_4$ mesophyll cells, not in the Calvin cycle.\nPhosphoglycolate is a product of photorespiration, not an acceptor.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which cells in a leaf contain a large number of chloroplasts and are the main sites of photosynthesis?","image":"","options":["Mesophyll cells","Epidermal cells","Xylem vessels","Phloem sieve tubes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the mesophyll cells of leaves have a large number of chloroplasts.\nThese cells are therefore responsible for CO2 fixation.\nEpidermal cells (other than guard cells) generally lack chloroplasts.\nXylem vessels and sieve tubes are conducting elements, not photosynthetic cells.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Calvin cycle, which compound is the first stable product formed when $CO_2$ is fixed?","image":"","options":["Oxaloacetic acid (OAA)","Ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP)","3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA)","Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Calvin identified the first $CO_2$ fixation product as 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), a 3-carbon acid.\nThis is why the pathway is called the C3 pathway.\nOAA is the first product in C4 plants.\nRuBP is the $CO_2$ acceptor in the Calvin cycle, and PEP is the acceptor in C4 mesophyll cells. Neither is a product.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) About 360 µL/L\n(b) Beyond 450 µL/L\n(c) Up to 0.05 per cent\n(d) Tomatoes and bell pepper\nList-II\n(i) CO2 level at which C3 plants show saturation\n(ii) Greenhouse crops grown in CO2-enriched atmosphere\n(iii) CO2 level at which C4 plants show saturation\n(iv) Rise in atmospheric CO2 that can increase fixation rates","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) C4 plants saturate at about 360 µL/L, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) C3 plants saturate only beyond 450 µL/L, so (b)-(i).\n(c) Raising CO2 up to 0.05 per cent increases fixation, though higher levels can damage over long periods, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Tomatoes and bell pepper give higher yields in CO2-enriched greenhouses, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The light reaction produces three products. Which one is NOT used in the biosynthetic phase and instead diffuses out of the chloroplast?","image":"","options":["$O_2$","ATP","NADPH","Both ATP and NADPH"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The light reaction produces ATP, NADPH and $O_2$.\nATP and NADPH are used in the stroma to fix $CO_2$ and make sugars.\n$O_2$ is not needed in the biosynthetic phase. It diffuses out of the chloroplast.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Photorespiration does not occur in C$_4$ plants.\nReason (R): The mesophyll cells of C$_4$ plants lack the enzyme RuBisCO.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that photorespiration does not occur in C$_4$ plants.\nR is true. C$_4$ mesophyll cells have PEPcase and lack RuBisCO.\nHowever, R does not explain A. The bundle sheath cells do contain RuBisCO and could, in principle, photorespire.\nPhotorespiration is avoided because C$_4$ acids are broken down in the bundle sheath to release CO$_2$. This raises the CO$_2$ concentration at RuBisCO, so the enzyme works as a carboxylase rather than an oxygenase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although chlorophyll a absorbs mainly blue and red light, some photosynthesis also occurs at other wavelengths of the visible spectrum.\nReason (R): Accessory pigments absorb light of other wavelengths and transfer the energy to chlorophyll a.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The action spectrum shows that most photosynthesis occurs in the blue and red regions, but some occurs at other visible wavelengths.\nR is true. Chlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids absorb light that chlorophyll a absorbs poorly and pass this energy to chlorophyll a.\nThis energy transfer widens the range of wavelengths that can drive photosynthesis. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following directly contribute to building up the proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane?\n(i) Splitting of water on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane\n(ii) An H-carrier that picks up a proton from the stroma while carrying an electron\n(iii) Use of stromal protons by NADP reductase to reduce NADP$^+$\n(iv) Conformational change in the CF$_1$ particle\n(v) Diffusion of O$_2$ out of the chloroplast","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Contributes: protons from water splitting are released into the lumen.\n(ii) Contributes: the H-carrier removes protons from the stroma and releases them into the lumen.\n(iii) Contributes: NADP reductase removes protons from the stroma.\n(iv) Does not contribute: the CF$_1$ change is driven by the breakdown of the gradient, not its build-up.\n(v) Does not contribute: O$_2$ diffusion has no role in the gradient.\nSo three items contribute.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the KOH-soaked cotton experiment, the part of the leaf kept inside the test tube tests negative for starch.\nReason (R): Carbon dioxide is absorbed by KOH, so photosynthesis cannot occur in the enclosed part of the leaf.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the enclosed portion shows no starch, while the part exposed to air tests positive.\nR is true: KOH removes CO2, and without CO2 the leaf cannot make carbohydrate.\nThe lack of CO2 is exactly why no starch forms in the enclosed part, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The stroma lamellae are considered a likely site of cyclic, rather than non-cyclic, photophosphorylation.\nReason (R): The membranes of stroma lamellae lack PS II as well as the NADP reductase enzyme.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT names the stroma lamellae as a possible location of cyclic photophosphorylation.\nR is true: grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II, but stroma lamellae lack PS II and NADP reductase.\nR explains A. Without PS II, electrons cannot flow non-cyclically from water through both photosystems. Without NADP reductase, NADP$^+$ cannot be reduced. So electrons from PS I can only cycle back, which gives ATP alone.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are external factors affecting photosynthesis?\nAvailability of sunlight, temperature, number of leaves, $CO_2$ concentration in air, amount of chlorophyll, water, internal $CO_2$ concentration","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"External factors: sunlight, temperature, atmospheric $CO_2$ concentration and water. That makes 4.\nInternal (plant) factors: number of leaves, amount of chlorophyll and internal $CO_2$ concentration. These depend on the plant's genetic predisposition and growth.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Chloroplasts in mesophyll cells align themselves along the cell walls to receive an optimum quantity of incident light. Which of the following correctly describes this alignment?","image":"","options":["In weak light, chloroplasts turn edge-on to the light; in very strong light, they turn their flat surfaces to face it","Chloroplasts remain fixed in one position regardless of light intensity","In weak light, flat surfaces face the light (perpendicular to it); in very strong light, they lie edge-on (parallel to it)","Chloroplasts always gather around the central vacuole, away from the cell walls"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that chloroplasts align along mesophyll cell walls to obtain optimum light.\nIn weak light, presenting the flat surface perpendicular to the incident light maximises absorption.\nIn very intense light, turning edge-on (flat surfaces parallel to the light) reduces exposure. This helps prevent chlorophyll breakdown.\nOption (1) reverses this response. Option (2) is wrong because chloroplasts can reposition. Option (4) is wrong because they lie along the cell walls.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Because the first product of $CO_2$ fixation was a 3-carbon acid, scientists spent many years searching for a 2-carbon $CO_2$ acceptor.\nStatement II: The acceptor eventually discovered, RuBP, is a 5-carbon aldose sugar.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Scientists reasoned that a 2C compound plus one $CO_2$ would give the 3C PGA, so they looked for a 2C acceptor for years.\nStatement II is incorrect. RuBP does have 5 carbons, but it is a ketose sugar, not an aldose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"By convention, the reactions of photosynthesis that are not directly driven by light but use ATP and NADPH are also called:","image":"","options":["Photochemical reactions","Hill reactions","Carbon reactions","Photolytic reactions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reactions driven directly by light are called light reactions or photochemical reactions.\nReactions that depend on ATP and NADPH but are not directly light driven are called dark reactions, or carbon reactions.\nThe name 'carbon reactions' fits because these reactions fix CO2 into sugars.\n'Photochemical' is wrong because it is the other name for the light reactions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Carboxylation\n(b) Reduction\n(c) Regeneration\n(d) RuBisCO\nList-II\n(i) Uses 1 ATP per $CO_2$ to re-form the $CO_2$ acceptor\n(ii) Enzyme with both carboxylase and oxygenase activity\n(iii) Fixation of $CO_2$ into a stable organic intermediate\n(iv) Uses 2 ATP and 2 NADPH per $CO_2$ fixed","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Carboxylation fixes $CO_2$ onto RuBP, forming two molecules of 3-PGA. This matches (iii).\n(b) Reduction uses 2 ATP and 2 NADPH for each $CO_2$. This matches (iv).\n(c) Regeneration uses 1 ATP to re-form RuBP. This matches (i).\n(d) RuBisCO can both carboxylate and oxygenate RuBP. This matches (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an experiment, part of an attached leaf is enclosed in a test tube containing cotton soaked in KOH, while the rest of the leaf is exposed to air. The set-up is kept in light. What is the role of the KOH-soaked cotton?","image":"","options":["It supplies extra oxygen to the enclosed part of the leaf","It absorbs carbon dioxide from the air inside the tube","It absorbs light so the enclosed part stays in darkness","It destroys chlorophyll in the enclosed part of the leaf"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"KOH absorbs CO2, so the air in the tube has no CO2 available.\nThe enclosed part still receives light and still has chlorophyll. The only factor that changes is CO2.\nThe tube is transparent, so option (3) is wrong: the leaf inside is not kept in darkness.\nKOH does not supply O2 and does not destroy chlorophyll.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are products of the light reaction of photosynthesis?\nATP, NADPH, O2, 3-PGA, glucose, RuBP","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the products of the light reaction as ATP, NADPH and O2.\nO2 diffuses out of the chloroplast. ATP and NADPH are used in the stroma.\n3-PGA, glucose and RuBP are intermediates or products of the Calvin cycle (biosynthetic phase). They are not light-reaction products.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In C$_4$ plants the Calvin cycle takes place in the mesophyll cells.\nReason (R): The mesophyll cells of C$_4$ plants lack the enzyme RuBisCO.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. In C$_4$ plants the Calvin cycle does not occur in the mesophyll cells. It occurs only in the bundle sheath cells.\nR is true. NCERT stresses that C$_4$ mesophyll cells lack RuBisCO and contain PEPcase instead.\nThe absence of RuBisCO is actually the reason the Calvin cycle cannot run in these cells.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Photosystem II passes electrons to Photosystem I during non-cyclic electron flow, even though it carries the number 'II'.\nReason (R): The photosystems were numbered in the order in which they were discovered, not in the order in which they work.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the Z scheme, excited electrons from PS II travel through the electron transport chain to PS I. So PS II functions first.\nR is true. NCERT says the photosystems are named in the sequence of their discovery.\nR explains A: the numbers come from discovery order, so the first-acting photosystem can carry the number 'II'.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"During light reaction in photosynthesis the following are formed:","image":"","options":["ATP and sugar","Hydrogen, O$_2$ and sugar","ATP, hydrogen and O$_2$","ATP, hydrogen and O$_2$ donor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The light reaction uses light to make ATP by photophosphorylation.\nIt also makes reducing power, NADPH (hydrogen carried by NADP).\nO$_2$ is released from the photolysis of water.\nSugar is made only in the Calvin cycle (dark reaction), so options (1) and (2) are wrong.\nIn option (4), water is the O$_2$ source, and an 'O$_2$ donor' is not a product.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) All organisms other than green plants that depend on them for food are heterotrophs.\n(ii) Photosynthesis is a physico-chemical process in which light energy drives the synthesis of organic compounds.\n(iii) In a leaf partially covered with black paper and kept in light, the covered portion tests positive for starch.\n(iv) In the KOH experiment, the part of the leaf exposed to air tests positive for starch.\n(v) Ultimately, all living forms on earth depend on sunlight for energy.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: organisms that depend on green plants for food are heterotrophs.\n(ii) Correct: this is how NCERT describes photosynthesis.\n(iii) Incorrect: the covered portion gets no light, so it cannot photosynthesise and lacks starch.\n(iv) Correct: the exposed part has CO2 available and forms starch.\n(v) Correct: all food chains rest on photosynthesis, which depends on sunlight.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which light range is least effective in photosynthesis?","image":"","options":["Blue","Green","Red","Violet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The absorption spectra of chlorophylls a and b peak in the blue (and violet) and red regions.\nGreen light is mostly reflected or transmitted, which is why leaves look green. Very little of it is absorbed, so it drives the least photosynthesis.\nRed and blue are the most effective regions, and violet is also well absorbed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The correct sequence of flow of electrons in the light reaction is","image":"","options":["PSII, plastoquinone, cytochromes, PSI, ferredoxin","PSI, plastoquinone, cytochromes, PSII, ferredoxin","PSI, ferredoxin, PSII,","PSI, plastoquinone, cytochromes, PSII, ferredoxin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the Z-scheme, electrons leave PSII (P680) after it is excited.\nThey pass down the chain through plastoquinone, then the cytochrome b$_6$f complex (and plastocyanin), to PSI (P700).\nFrom PSI, the re-excited electrons go to ferredoxin and then reduce NADP$^+$ to NADPH.\nOptions (2) and (4) begin at PSI, so they reverse the order. Option (3) is incomplete.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For many years, scientists tried to find a 2-carbon compound as the primary $CO_2$ acceptor in the Calvin cycle.\nReason (R): The first product of $CO_2$ fixation was known to be a 3-carbon acid, so they assumed that a 2-carbon acceptor plus one $CO_2$ formed it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that scientists spent many years looking for a 2-carbon acceptor before they discovered RuBP.\nR is true. Their reasoning was simple arithmetic: 2C + one $CO_2$ = 3C (PGA).\nR is exactly why they searched for a 2C compound, so R explains A.\nIn reality, the 5C RuBP plus $CO_2$ gives two molecules of 3C PGA.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the role of the CF$_0$ part of ATP synthase in the thylakoid membrane?","image":"","options":["It splits water to release protons into the lumen","It forms a transmembrane channel through which protons undergo facilitated diffusion","It reduces NADP$^+$ to NADPH + H$^+$","It actively pumps protons from the stroma into the lumen using ATP"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"CF$_0$ is embedded in the thylakoid membrane.\nIt forms a transmembrane channel that carries out facilitated diffusion of protons across the membrane, down their gradient.\nCF$_0$ does not use ATP to pump protons; protons flow through it passively.\nWater splitting belongs to PS II, and NADP$^+$ is reduced by NADP reductase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Photophosphorylation in the stroma lamellae yields ATP but no NADPH + H$^+$.\nReason (R): Stroma lamellae membranes lack PS II as well as the NADP reductase enzyme, so excited electrons from PS I cycle back to it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cyclic photophosphorylation, thought to occur in the stroma lamellae, produces only ATP.\nR is true. Stroma lamellae lack PS II and NADP reductase. The electron excited at P700 therefore does not reach NADP$^+$. Instead, it returns to PS I through the electron transport chain.\nThis is exactly why no NADPH forms, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Isolated chloroplasts are illuminated only with light of wavelengths longer than 680 nm. Which of the following outcomes is expected?","image":"","options":["Both photosystems are excited, and ATP, NADPH and O$_2$ are all produced","Only PS II is excited, so water is split but no ATP is formed","Only PS I is effectively excited, so cyclic flow produces ATP but neither NADPH nor O$_2$","Only PS I is excited, so NADPH is formed but ATP synthesis stops"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: The PS II reaction centre is P680 and the PS I reaction centre is P700. Light beyond 680 nm effectively excites only PS I.\nStep 2: Without PS II, water is not split. So no O$_2$ is released and no electrons flow from water through the Z scheme.\nStep 3: NCERT states that cyclic photophosphorylation occurs when only light beyond 680 nm is available.\nStep 4: In this cyclic flow, electrons return to PS I. ATP is formed, but NADPH is not, because NADP$^+$ is not reduced.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about bundle sheath cells of a $C_4$ leaf with Kranz anatomy are correct?\n(i) They have a large number of chloroplasts.\n(ii) Their walls are thick and impervious to gaseous exchange.\n(iii) They have no intercellular spaces.\n(iv) They are rich in PEP carboxylase.\n(v) The Calvin cycle occurs in them.\n(vi) They may form several layers around the vascular bundles.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i), (ii), (iii) Correct: NCERT lists many chloroplasts, thick walls impervious to gases and no intercellular spaces.\n(iv) Incorrect: bundle sheath cells are rich in RuBisCO but lack PEPcase.\n(v) Correct: in $C_4$ plants the Calvin cycle occurs only in bundle sheath cells.\n(vi) Correct: the cells may form several layers.\nSo five statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'Kranz' used for the leaf anatomy of C4 plants such as maize literally means:","image":"","options":["Wreath","Crown of thorns","Double layer","Green sheath"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Kranz means 'wreath'.\nThe name reflects how the large bundle sheath cells are arranged in a ring around each vascular bundle.\n'Green sheath' is tempting because these cells have many chloroplasts. However, that is not what the word means.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Isolated, intact chloroplasts are supplied with ADP, inorganic phosphate, NADP$^+$ and water. They are then illuminated only with light of wavelength 700 nm. Which set of observations is expected?","image":"","options":["ATP, NADPH and O$_2$ are all formed","NADPH and O$_2$ are formed but no ATP","ATP and O$_2$ are formed but no NADPH","ATP is formed, but neither NADPH nor O$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Light of 700 nm lies beyond 680 nm. It excites P700 (PS I) but not P680 (PS II).\nWith PS II inactive, water is not split, so no O$_2$ is released and no electrons enter the chain from water.\nThe electrons of PS I therefore cycle back through the electron transport chain. This is cyclic photophosphorylation.\nCyclic flow makes ATP but does not reduce NADP$^+$.\nOption (3) fails because O$_2$ evolution depends on PS II.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Photosystem I and Photosystem II are named in the sequence of their discovery, not in the sequence in which they function during the light reaction.\nStatement II: The reaction centre chlorophyll a of Photosystem I has its absorption peak at 680 nm.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states the photosystems are named in the order they were discovered. In the light reaction, PS II actually acts before PS I.\nStatement II is incorrect. The reaction centre of PS I is P700, which absorbs maximally at 700 nm. P680 is the reaction centre of PS II.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the C4 pathway are correct?\n(i) The primary $CO_2$ acceptor is a 5-carbon compound.\n(ii) Oxaloacetic acid is formed in the mesophyll cells.\n(iii) Bundle sheath cells lack PEP carboxylase.\n(iv) The 3-carbon molecule formed after decarboxylation is converted back to PEP within the bundle sheath cells.\n(v) Plants with this pathway are adapted to dry tropical regions.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect. The acceptor is PEP, which has 3 carbons.\n(ii) Correct. PEPcase forms OAA in the mesophyll.\n(iii) Correct. Bundle sheath cells have RuBisCO but lack PEPcase.\n(iv) Incorrect. The 3-carbon molecule is transported back to the mesophyll, and PEP is regenerated there.\n(v) Correct. NCERT links C4 plants to dry tropical regions.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a $C_3$ chloroplast, RuBisCO acts on 10 molecules of RuBP. It carboxylates 8 of them and oxygenates the remaining 2. How many molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate are formed in total?","image":"","options":["20","18","16","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 8 RuBP are carboxylated and 2 RuBP are oxygenated.\nCarboxylation: RuBP + $CO_2$ gives 2 PGA, so 8 × 2 = 16 PGA.\nOxygenation: RuBP + $O_2$ gives 1 phosphoglycerate + 1 phosphoglycolate (2C), so 2 × 1 = 2 PGA (plus 2 phosphoglycolate).\nTotal PGA = 16 + 2 = 18.\nOption (1), 20, wrongly assumes that oxygenation also gives two PGA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In nature, the rate of photosynthesis stops rising with light intensity at a fairly low level of illumination. Light saturation of photosynthesis occurs at about what fraction of full sunlight?","image":"","options":["10 per cent","25 per cent","50 per cent","90 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At low light intensities, CO$_2$ fixation rises linearly with incident light.\nAt higher intensities the rate levels off because other factors, such as CO$_2$, become limiting.\nNCERT states that light saturation occurs at 10 per cent of full sunlight.\nSo, except for plants in shade or dense forests, light is rarely a limiting factor in nature. The higher values (25%, 50%, 90%) overestimate the saturation point.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Water affects photosynthesis more through its effect on the plant than directly as a reactant of the light reaction.\nReason (R): Water stress causes stomata to close, reducing $CO_2$ availability, and makes leaves wilt, reducing leaf surface area and metabolic activity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Water is a reactant in the light reaction, but NCERT says its effect as a factor is mainly through the plant.\nR is true. Stomatal closure cuts CO2 entry, and wilting reduces leaf area and metabolism.\nThese indirect effects are exactly why water acts mainly through the plant, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about ATP synthesis in chloroplasts are correct?\n(i) Protons accumulate in the thylakoid lumen.\n(ii) The CF$_1$ part of ATP synthase forms the transmembrane proton channel.\n(iii) NADP reductase is located on the stroma side of the thylakoid membrane.\n(iv) Water splitting occurs on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane.\n(v) The pH of the lumen rises as the proton gradient builds up.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Protons build up inside the lumen, unlike mitochondria, where they build up in the intermembrane space.\n(ii) Incorrect. CF$_0$ forms the channel. CF$_1$ protrudes into the stroma and undergoes the conformational change that makes ATP.\n(iii) Correct. NADP reductase lies on the stroma side and uses protons taken from the stroma.\n(iv) Correct. Water is split on the inner side, so its protons are released into the lumen.\n(v) Incorrect. Proton accumulation lowers the lumen pH.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Cyclic photophosphorylation results in the formation of:","image":"","options":["ATP only","NADPH + H$^+$ only","Both ATP and NADPH + H$^+$","ATP, NADPH + H$^+$ and O$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In cyclic flow, only PS I works.\nThe excited electron is not passed on to NADP$^+$. It returns to the PS I complex through the electron transport chain.\nATP is made during this cyclic flow, but NADP$^+$ is not reduced, so no NADPH is formed.\nPS II is not involved, so water is not split and no O$_2$ is released.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Hatch and Slack pathway, which molecule first accepts atmospheric $CO_2$ in the mesophyll cells of a C4 plant?","image":"","options":["Ribulose bisphosphate, a 5-carbon compound","Phosphoenol pyruvate, a 3-carbon compound","Oxaloacetic acid, a 4-carbon compound","3-phosphoglyceric acid, a 3-carbon compound"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In C4 plants, the primary $CO_2$ acceptor in the mesophyll cells is phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP), a 3-carbon molecule.\nThe enzyme PEP carboxylase (PEPcase) carries out this fixation.\nRuBP is the acceptor in the Calvin cycle. In C4 plants that cycle runs only in the bundle sheath cells.\nOAA is the product of PEP fixation, not the acceptor.\nPGA is the first product of the C3 pathway.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In C4 plants, the first stable product of $CO_2$ fixation is a 4-carbon acid, OAA.\n(ii) C4 plants do not use the Calvin cycle.\n(iii) PGA contains three carbon atoms.\n(iv) The Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants.\n(v) RuBP is a 3-carbon sugar.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","One","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: OAA is the C4 acid formed first in C4 plants.\n(ii) Incorrect: C4 plants use the Calvin cycle as their main biosynthetic pathway, in the bundle sheath cells.\n(iii) Correct: PGA is a C3 acid.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT says the Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants.\n(v) Incorrect: RuBP is a 5-carbon ketose sugar.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In T.W. Engelmann's experiment, a prism split light into its spectral components, and this light fell on the filamentous green alga Cladophora kept in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. In which regions of the spectrum did the bacteria accumulate mainly?","image":"","options":["Green and yellow","Blue and red","Violet and orange","Yellow and far-red"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The aerobic bacteria gathered where Cladophora released the most O$_2$, so they marked the sites of O$_2$ evolution.\nThey clustered mainly in the blue and red regions of the spectrum, giving the first action spectrum of photosynthesis.\nGreen light is largely reflected by chlorophyll, so very little O$_2$ is evolved in the green region. That rules out option (1).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Some photosynthesis takes place even at wavelengths of visible light where chlorophyll a absorbs poorly.\nReason (R): Chlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids absorb light and transfer the absorbed energy to chlorophyll a.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: most photosynthesis occurs in the blue and red regions, but some also occurs at other visible wavelengths.\nR is true: the accessory pigments absorb light and pass the energy on to chlorophyll a.\nR explains A: these pigments absorb wavelengths that chlorophyll a absorbs poorly, which widens the range of light that can be used.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The primary acceptor of $CO_2$ in the Calvin cycle is best described as:","image":"","options":["A 3-carbon compound, phosphoenol pyruvate","A 2-carbon compound that gives PGA on accepting $CO_2$","A 4-carbon organic acid, oxaloacetic acid","A 5-carbon ketose sugar, ribulose bisphosphate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Studies showed, unexpectedly, that the $CO_2$ acceptor is RuBP, a 5-carbon ketose sugar.\nScientists had expected a 2-carbon acceptor. No such compound was ever found.\nPEP is the 3-carbon acceptor in C4 mesophyll cells.\nOAA is a product of fixation, not an acceptor.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The correct overall equation of photosynthesis is written as $6CO_2 + 12H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6H_2O + 6O_2$. Why are 12 molecules of water shown as substrate instead of 6?","image":"","options":["Glucose contains 12 hydrogen atoms, which need 12 water molecules","All the O2 released comes from water, and 6 O2 needs 12 oxygen atoms, which only 12 H2O can supply","Six water molecules are used in the light reaction and six in the Calvin cycle","Twelve water molecules are needed to dissolve six CO2 molecules"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Since van Niel's work, we know that all the O2 released comes from water, not from CO2.\nSix O2 molecules contain 12 oxygen atoms, and each H2O gives one oxygen atom, so 12 H2O are needed.\nThe oxygen of CO2 ends up in glucose and in the 6 H2O formed, which is why water appears on both sides.\nOption (1) fails because 6 H2O already contain 12 H atoms, so hydrogen count alone does not explain 12 molecules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the light harvesting system of a photosystem is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Antenna pigments make photosynthesis more efficient by absorbing light of different wavelengths","Every chlorophyll a molecule of a photosystem belongs to its antennae","Energy trapped by antenna pigments is funnelled to the reaction centre chlorophyll a","Accessory pigments also protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: antenna pigments absorb different wavelengths, which increases efficiency.\n(2) Incorrect: the antennae include all pigments except one chlorophyll a molecule. That single molecule forms the reaction centre, so not every chlorophyll a belongs to the antennae.\n(3) Correct: absorbed energy is funnelled to the reaction centre chlorophyll.\n(4) Correct: accessory pigments protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) P680\n(b) P700\n(c) Antennae\n(d) Accessory pigments\nList-II\n(i) Reaction centre chlorophyll a absorbing maximally at 700 nm\n(ii) All pigments of a photosystem except one chlorophyll a molecule\n(iii) Reaction centre chlorophyll a of PS II absorbing red light at 680 nm\n(iv) Chlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids that pass absorbed energy to chlorophyll a","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) P680 is the PS II reaction centre, which absorbs red light at 680 nm. It matches (iii).\n(b) P700 is the PS I reaction centre, with its peak at 700 nm. It matches (i).\n(c) The antennae are all pigments of a photosystem except the single reaction-centre chlorophyll a. It matches (ii).\n(d) Accessory pigments (chlorophyll b, xanthophylls, carotenoids) transfer absorbed energy to chlorophyll a. It matches (iv).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about chemiosmotic ATP synthesis in chloroplasts is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The primary electron acceptor on the outer side of the membrane passes its electron to an H-carrier, which picks up a proton from the stroma","Energy released when the gradient breaks down causes a conformational change in CF$_1$, allowing several ATP molecules to be made","During ATP synthesis, protons diffuse through CF$_0$ from the stroma into the lumen","Chemiosmosis requires a membrane, a proton pump, a proton gradient and ATP synthase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the H-carrier takes a proton from the stroma and releases it into the lumen.\n(2) Correct: the breakdown of the gradient drives the CF$_1$ conformational change that produces ATP.\n(3) Incorrect: protons move down their gradient, from the lumen (high concentration) to the stroma through CF$_0$, not the other way.\n(4) Correct: these are the four requirements of chemiosmosis listed in NCERT.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In chloroplasts, ATP synthesis is linked to a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane, with protons accumulating in the lumen.\nStatement II: In mitochondria, protons accumulate in the matrix when electrons move through the electron transport system.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in photosynthesis, protons build up inside the thylakoid lumen.\nStatement II is incorrect: in respiration, protons accumulate in the intermembrane space of the mitochondria, not in the matrix.\nThis is the key difference NCERT points out between chemiosmosis in the two organelles.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Light saturation of photosynthesis in most plants occurs at about what fraction of full sunlight?","image":"","options":["50 per cent","10 per cent","90 per cent","25 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that light saturation occurs at about 10 per cent of full sunlight.\nThis is why light is rarely a limiting factor in nature, except for plants in shade or in dense forests.\nThe values 25, 50 and 90 per cent would wrongly make light limiting over most of the day.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Since the first stable product of $CO_2$ fixation in C4 plants is oxaloacetic acid, these plants do not use the Calvin cycle for sugar synthesis.\nStatement II: The bundle sheath cells of C4 plants have thick walls that are impervious to gaseous exchange.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. C4 plants do form OAA first, but they still use the Calvin cycle as their main biosynthetic pathway. It runs in their bundle sheath cells.\nNCERT notes that the Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants.\nStatement II is correct. Bundle sheath cells have thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Thylakoid membranes are separated from the stroma of chloroplasts. They are then illuminated in a medium containing water, ADP, inorganic phosphate and NADP+, but no stroma enzymes. Which outcome is expected?","image":"","options":["Sugar is synthesised, but no ATP or NADPH is formed","Only ATP is formed and no O2 is released, since O2 comes from CO2","No reaction occurs, because light reactions need stroma enzymes","ATP, NADPH and O2 are formed, but no sugar is synthesised"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The membrane system traps light, splits water and synthesises ATP and NADPH. It can therefore work without the stroma.\nWater splitting releases O2, because the O2 of photosynthesis comes from water, not CO2.\nSugar synthesis needs the stroma enzymes (and CO2), so no sugar forms.\nThis outcome demonstrates the division of labour between membranes and stroma.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cyclic photophosphorylation is believed to occur in chloroplasts in addition to non-cyclic photophosphorylation.\nReason (R): For every $CO_2$ fixed, the Calvin cycle uses 3 ATP but only 2 NADPH, so extra ATP has to be supplied.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cyclic photophosphorylation occurs, for example in the stroma lamellae, and it makes only ATP.\nR is true. Fixing each $CO_2$ needs 3 ATP and 2 NADPH, so more ATP than NADPH is required.\nNCERT states that cyclic phosphorylation probably takes place to meet this difference. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A C3 plant and a C4 plant are kept under high light intensity at 360 µL/L $CO_2$. The $CO_2$ concentration is then raised to 450 µL/L, with all other conditions unchanged. What is the most likely result?","image":"","options":["Both plants show a large increase in photosynthetic rate","Only the C4 plant shows a clear increase in photosynthetic rate","Neither plant responds, because light is now limiting","Only the C3 plant shows a clear increase in photosynthetic rate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"C4 plants are already saturated at about 360 µL/L, so extra CO2 gives them little gain.\nC3 plants keep responding to rising CO2 and saturate only beyond 450 µL/L, so their rate increases.\nBecause light is high, it is not limiting. Neither group responds to high CO2 under low light, but that does not apply here.\nThis is why current CO2 levels limit C3 plants.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Joseph Priestley\nB. Jan Ingenhousz\nC. Julius von Sachs\nD. T.W. Engelmann\nList-II\n(i) Described the first action spectrum of photosynthesis using Cladophora and aerobic bacteria\n(ii) Gave evidence that glucose is produced in green parts and is usually stored as starch\n(iii) Proposed that plants restore to the air whatever burning candles and breathing animals remove\n(iv) Showed that sunlight is essential for the plant process that purifies fouled air","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Priestley's bell jar experiments with a mint plant led him to propose that plants restore the air.\nB-(iv): Ingenhousz repeated the set-up in dark and in sunlight. He showed that light is needed and that only green parts release oxygen bubbles.\nC-(ii): Sachs (1854) showed that glucose is made in green parts and stored as starch.\nD-(i): Engelmann used prism-split light on Cladophora with aerobic bacteria. The bacteria gathered in the blue and red regions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Photosystem I and Photosystem II are named on the basis of:","image":"","options":["The order in which they function in the Z scheme","The sequence of their discovery","The wavelength absorbed by their reaction centres, the shorter wavelength being PS I","The number of pigment molecules in their antennae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that PS I and PS II are named in the sequence of their discovery. They are not named in the sequence in which they function.\nIn the Z scheme, PS II actually acts first and PS I second, so option (1) is wrong.\nThe PS I reaction centre is P700, a longer wavelength than P680 of PS II, so option (3) is also wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Primary $CO_2$ acceptor in C4 plants\nB. Primary $CO_2$ acceptor in C3 plants\nC. First stable product of $CO_2$ fixation in C4 plants\nD. Site of the Calvin cycle in C4 plants\nList-II\n(i) Bundle sheath cells\n(ii) Phosphoenol pyruvate\n(iii) Oxaloacetic acid\n(iv) Ribulose bisphosphate\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A–(ii): In C4 plants, the 3-carbon PEP accepts $CO_2$ in the mesophyll cells, using PEPcase.\nB–(iv): In C3 plants, the 5-carbon RuBP is the acceptor, using RuBisCO.\nC–(iii): The first stable product in C4 plants is the 4-carbon OAA.\nD–(i): In C4 plants, the Calvin cycle runs only in the bundle sheath cells, which are rich in RuBisCO. The mesophyll cells of C4 plants lack RuBisCO.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"An electron micrograph of a chloroplast section is labelled. How many of the following are shown as structures present inside the chloroplast?\nRibosomes, starch granule, lipid droplet, grana, stroma lamellae, nucleolus, centriole","image":"","options":["5","4","6","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The NCERT diagram of a chloroplast labels ribosomes, a starch granule, a lipid droplet, grana and stromal lamellae. These five are present.\nThe nucleolus lies inside the nucleus, not the chloroplast.\nCentrioles are absent from the cells of higher plants, and they are never part of a chloroplast.\nSo 5 structures are present.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the C4 pathway are correct?\n(i) PEP carboxylase is abundant in the bundle sheath cells.\n(ii) C4 plants are typically adapted to dry tropical regions.\n(iii) In the bundle sheath, C4 acids are broken down to release $CO_2$ and a 3-carbon molecule.\n(iv) The Hatch and Slack pathway is a linear, non-cyclic process.\n(v) Sorghum leaves show Kranz anatomy.\n(vi) Oxaloacetic acid is converted to malic acid inside the bundle sheath cells.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: bundle sheath cells lack PEPcase. They are rich in RuBisCO.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT links C4 plants to dry tropical regions.\n(iii) Correct: decarboxylation of C4 acids in the bundle sheath releases $CO_2$ and a 3C molecule.\n(iv) Incorrect: the pathway is cyclic, because PEP is regenerated.\n(v) Correct: sorghum and maize show Kranz anatomy.\n(vi) Incorrect: OAA forms malic or aspartic acid in the mesophyll itself.\nCorrect statements: (ii), (iii) and (v), which makes three.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about electron flow during the light reaction is correct?","image":"","options":["Electrons lost by the PS I reaction centre are replaced directly by electrons obtained from splitting of water","Electrons are used up by the cytochromes, so PS II needs a continuous supply of new electrons","After excitation at P700, electrons pass to an acceptor and then move downhill to NADP$^+$, reducing it to NADPH + H$^+$","Movement of electrons from the PS II acceptor through the cytochromes to PS I is uphill on the redox potential scale"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: water splitting replaces the electrons of PS II. PS I's electrons are replaced by those coming from PS II.\n(2) Incorrect: electrons are not used up in the transport chain. PS II needs replacement electrons because it hands its electrons on to PS I.\n(3) Correct: this is the final arm of the Z scheme, ending in NADPH + H$^+$.\n(4) Incorrect: this movement is downhill. The uphill steps occur only on excitation at PS II and at PS I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A green leaf receives optimal light and optimal $CO_2$, yet it does not photosynthesise. Its temperature is very low. Which statement best interprets this situation?","image":"","options":["Light is the limiting factor, because light reactions are the most temperature sensitive","Temperature is the limiting factor; raising it to the optimum will start photosynthesis, as Blackman's law predicts","CO2 is still limiting, because CO2 is always the limiting factor in nature","Chlorophyll has broken down, so no change in conditions can restore photosynthesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Blackman's law says the rate is set by the factor nearest its minimal value. Here, that factor is temperature.\nThe enzymatic dark reactions need a suitable temperature, and NCERT says such a leaf starts photosynthesising at the optimal temperature.\nLight and CO2 are already optimal, so neither can be limiting.\nLight reactions are less temperature sensitive than dark reactions, not more.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In Engelmann's experiment, a filament of the green alga Cladophora was illuminated with light split by a prism and kept in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. What was the role of the aerobic bacteria?","image":"","options":["To supply CO2 to the alga for photosynthesis","To detect the sites where O2 was being evolved","To absorb the green light falling on the alga","To break down the starch formed by the alga"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aerobic bacteria need oxygen, so they gather wherever O2 is being released.\nEngelmann used them as living indicators to detect the sites of O2 evolution along the alga.\nOption (1) is tempting because bacteria do respire and release CO2, but that was not their purpose in the experiment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A maize leaf is briefly exposed to $^{14}CO_2$ in light, and the location of the radioactive label is tracked over time. Which option correctly identifies (a) the compound and cell in which the label first appears, and (b) the cell in which labelled 3-PGA is subsequently formed?","image":"","options":["(a) 3-PGA in mesophyll cells; (b) mesophyll cells","(a) OAA in bundle sheath cells; (b) mesophyll cells","(a) OAA in mesophyll cells; (b) bundle sheath cells","(a) PEP in mesophyll cells; (b) bundle sheath cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Maize is a C4 plant. PEPcase fixes $^{14}CO_2$ onto PEP in the mesophyll, so the label first appears in OAA in the mesophyll cells.\nLabelled C4 acids then move to the bundle sheath. There they are decarboxylated, and the released $^{14}CO_2$ is fixed by RuBisCO into 3-PGA.\nMesophyll cells lack RuBisCO, so they cannot form PGA. This rules out options (1) and (2).\nPEP is the acceptor, not the product, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A graph shows the action spectrum of photosynthesis superimposed on the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a. Near 550 nm, the absorption curve of chlorophyll a is close to zero, yet the action spectrum shows a small but clear rate of photosynthesis. The best explanation for this is that:","image":"","options":["Chlorophyll a absorbs green light strongly but cannot release electrons at this wavelength","Accessory pigments absorb light at these wavelengths and pass the energy to chlorophyll a","Dark reactions run independently of light at these wavelengths","Green light is absorbed directly by RuBisCO in the stroma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chlorophyll a absorbs very little light around 550 nm, so it cannot account for the photosynthesis seen there.\nAccessory pigments (chlorophyll b, xanthophylls and carotenoids) absorb other wavelengths and transfer the energy to chlorophyll a.\nOption 3 is wrong because the carbon reactions depend on ATP and NADPH made in the light reactions.\nOption 4 is wrong because RuBisCO is an enzyme, not a pigment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The electron transfer pathway of non-cyclic photophosphorylation is called the 'Z scheme'. Its characteristic Z shape appears when the electron carriers are arranged in sequence on a:","image":"","options":["wavelength (absorption) scale","pH scale of the thylakoid lumen","redox potential scale","time scale of their discovery"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says the Z shape forms when all the carriers are placed in sequence on a redox (oxidation-reduction) potential scale.\nOn this scale, electrons go uphill when excited at PS II and PS I. They move downhill through the cytochromes and finally to NADP+.\nOrder of discovery explains only how the photosystems are numbered. It does not give the Z shape.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the splitting of water in the light reaction:\n(i) It is associated with Photosystem II.\n(ii) It occurs on the inner (lumen) side of the thylakoid membrane.\n(iii) It supplies electrons that directly replace those lost from the PS I reaction centre.\n(iv) It is the source of the O2 released in photosynthesis.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii) and (iii) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the water-splitting complex is associated with PS II.\n(ii) Correct: PS II and its water-splitting complex lie on the inner side of the thylakoid membrane, so protons collect in the lumen.\n(iii) Incorrect: electrons from water replace those removed from PS II. PS I receives its replacement electrons from PS II through the electron transport chain.\n(iv) Correct: 2H2O → 4H+ + O2 + 4e−, so the released O2 comes from water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about cyclic photophosphorylation are correct?\n(i) Only PS I is functional.\n(ii) The excited electron is returned to PS I through the electron transport chain.\n(iii) Oxygen is released as a by-product.\n(iv) It can occur when only light of wavelengths beyond 680 nm is available.\n(v) NADPH + H$^+$ is produced along with ATP.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: only PS I works in cyclic flow.\n(ii) Correct: the electron cycles back to the PS I complex through the electron transport chain.\n(iii) Incorrect: water splitting belongs to PS II, which is not involved, so no O2 is released.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT states cyclic flow occurs with light beyond 680 nm.\n(v) Incorrect: only ATP is formed, with no NADPH.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the scientists in List-I with their contributions in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Joseph Priestley\nB. Jan Ingenhousz\nC. T.W. Engelmann\nD. Cornelius van Niel\nList-II\n(i) Studied purple and green bacteria and inferred that the $O_2$ released by green plants comes from water\n(ii) Described the first action spectrum of photosynthesis using Cladophora and aerobic bacteria\n(iii) Proposed that plants restore to the air whatever burning candles and breathing animals remove\n(iv) Showed that sunlight is essential and that oxygen bubbles form only around green parts of an aquatic plant\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-iii: Priestley's bell jar experiments with a mint plant showed that plants restore the air.\nB-iv: Ingenhousz compared set-ups kept in light and in dark. He also saw oxygen bubbles only around green parts in bright light.\nC-ii: Engelmann split light with a prism. Aerobic bacteria gathered in the blue and red regions, giving the first action spectrum.\nD-i: van Niel's work on bacteria led him to infer that the evolved $O_2$ comes from $H_2O$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Greenhouse crops such as tomato and bell pepper give higher yields when grown in a carbon dioxide-enriched atmosphere.\nReason (R): C$_3$ plants show $CO_2$ saturation only beyond about 450 µl L$^{-1}$, so present atmospheric $CO_2$ levels limit their photosynthesis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT cites tomato and bell pepper as greenhouse crops grown in $CO_2$-enriched air for higher yield.\nR is true. C$_4$ plants saturate at about 360 µl L$^{-1}$, whereas C$_3$ plants keep responding until beyond 450 µl L$^{-1}$. Current $CO_2$ levels therefore limit C$_3$ plants.\nThese crops are C$_3$ plants, so extra $CO_2$ raises their photosynthesis and productivity. R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A green plant is supplied with CO2 in which the oxygen atoms are the heavy isotope $^{18}O$, along with ordinary water. Based on the correct equation $6CO_2 + 12H_2O \\rightarrow C_6H_{12}O_6 + 6H_2O + 6O_2$, where would the $^{18}O$ label appear?","image":"","options":["Only in the O2 released","In the glucose and the newly formed water, but not in the O2 released","In the O2 released and in the glucose only","Equally in the O2, glucose and newly formed water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The O2 released comes entirely from water, as van Niel inferred and radioisotope techniques later proved.\nThe water supplied here is unlabelled, so the released O2 carries no 18O.\nThe 12 oxygen atoms of 6 CO2 must go to the other products: 6 into glucose and 6 into the 6 H2O formed.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) wrongly assume that oxygen from CO2 enters the released O2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about light as a factor in photosynthesis are correct?\n(i) Light quality, light intensity and duration of exposure must be distinguished when light is discussed as a factor.\n(ii) At low light intensities, the rate of CO2 fixation rises linearly with incident light.\n(iii) At high light intensities, the rate keeps rising in proportion to light because no other factor becomes limiting.\n(iv) Increasing incident light beyond a point can break down chlorophyll and decrease photosynthesis.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT asks us to separate quality, intensity and duration of light.\n(ii) Correct: the response is linear at low intensities.\n(iii) Incorrect: at higher intensities the rate levels off because other factors become limiting.\n(iv) Correct: excess light causes chlorophyll breakdown and lowers photosynthesis.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (components of the Hatch and Slack pathway) with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP)\nB. Oxaloacetic acid (OAA)\nC. Malic acid / aspartic acid\nD. RuBisCO\nList-II\n(i) Transported from mesophyll to bundle sheath cells\n(ii) 3-carbon primary $CO_2$ acceptor present in mesophyll cells\n(iii) First stable 4-carbon product formed in mesophyll cells\n(iv) Rich in bundle sheath cells but absent from mesophyll cells","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-ii: PEP is the 3C primary acceptor, and PEPcase fixes $CO_2$ in the mesophyll.\nB-iii: OAA is the first stable C$_4$ product and forms in the mesophyll.\nC-i: OAA is converted to malic or aspartic acid, which moves to the bundle sheath cells. There it is broken down to release $CO_2$.\nD-iv: RuBisCO is abundant in bundle sheath cells, while mesophyll cells lack it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the leaf pigments in List-I with the colours they show on a paper chromatogram in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Chlorophyll a\n(b) Chlorophyll b\n(c) Xanthophylls\n(d) Carotenoids\nList-II\n(i) Yellow to yellow-orange\n(ii) Yellow green\n(iii) Bright or blue green\n(iv) Yellow","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Chlorophyll a, the chief pigment, appears bright or blue green, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Chlorophyll b appears yellow green, so it matches (ii).\n(c) Xanthophylls appear yellow, so they match (iv).\n(d) Carotenoids appear yellow to yellow-orange, so they match (i).\nThe most tempting mistake is swapping the two chlorophylls. Chlorophyll a is the bluer green and chlorophyll b the yellower green.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an internal (plant) factor affecting the rate of photosynthesis?","image":"","options":["Availability of sunlight","Atmospheric temperature","Number, size and orientation of leaves","Availability of water in the soil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Internal factors are those that belong to the plant itself and depend on its genetic predisposition and growth.\nThey include:\n- the number, size, age and orientation of leaves;\n- mesophyll cells and chloroplasts;\n- internal CO2 concentration;\n- the amount of chlorophyll.\nSunlight, temperature, CO2 concentration and water are external factors.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the bundle sheath cells of a C4 leaf such as maize is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They have a large number of chloroplasts","Their walls are thick and impervious to gaseous exchange","They have large intercellular spaces for rapid diffusion of $CO_2$","They may form several layers around the vascular bundles"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes Kranz (wreath) anatomy, in which large bundle sheath cells surround the vascular bundles.\n(1) Correct: these cells are rich in chloroplasts.\n(2) Correct: their walls are thick and impervious to gases.\n(4) Correct: they may form several layers around the bundles.\n(3) Incorrect: bundle sheath cells have NO intercellular spaces. This helps keep $CO_2$ concentrated around RuBisCO.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Photorespiration does not occur in $C_4$ plants.\nReason (R): The mesophyll cells of $C_4$ plants lack PEP carboxylase, so $CO_2$ is fixed directly by RuBisCO in the bundle sheath.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. $C_4$ acids are broken down in the bundle sheath cells, which raises the $CO_2$ concentration at the RuBisCO site. RuBisCO then works mainly as a carboxylase.\nR is false. Mesophyll cells of $C_4$ plants contain PEPcase and lack RuBisCO. Bundle sheath cells contain RuBisCO and lack PEPcase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In $C_4$ plants, the Calvin cycle takes place in the mesophyll cells.\nStatement II: The bundle sheath cells of $C_4$ plants are rich in RuBisCO but lack PEPcase.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In $C_4$ plants the mesophyll cells lack RuBisCO, so the Calvin cycle runs only in the bundle sheath cells.\nStatement II is correct. Bundle sheath cells are rich in RuBisCO and lack PEPcase.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution):\nList-I\n(A) Joseph Priestley\n(B) Jan Ingenhousz\n(C) Julius von Sachs\n(D) Cornelius van Niel\nList-II\n(i) Showed that glucose is made in green parts and is usually stored as starch\n(ii) Proposed that plants restore to the air what breathing animals and burning candles remove\n(iii) Proposed that hydrogen from an oxidisable compound reduces CO$_2$ to carbohydrate\n(iv) Showed that sunlight is essential for the plant to purify foul air\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-ii: Priestley's bell jar with a mint plant kept the mouse alive and the candle burning, so he said plants restore the air.\nB-iv: Ingenhousz repeated the setup in dark and in sunlight and showed that light is essential.\nC-i: Sachs gave evidence for glucose production, its storage as starch, and chlorophyll in chloroplasts.\nD-iii: van Niel proposed the general equation 2H$_2$A + CO$_2$ → 2A + CH$_2$O + H$_2$O from his work on bacteria.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The Calvin cycle uses ATP and NADPH in a 3 : 2 ratio for each $CO_2$ fixed. According to NCERT, which process probably meets this extra ATP demand, and why is it suited to the job?","image":"","options":["Non-cyclic photophosphorylation, because it makes ATP without making NADPH","Cyclic photophosphorylation, because electrons cycle back to PS I, so only ATP is made and no NADPH","Photorespiration, because it releases $CO_2$ along with net synthesis of ATP","The regeneration stage of the Calvin cycle, because it produces one ATP per RuBP formed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cycle needs 3 ATP but only 2 NADPH per $CO_2$, so it needs extra ATP.\nIn cyclic photophosphorylation, only PS I works. The excited electron returns to PS I instead of reducing $NADP^+$, so only ATP is made.\nNon-cyclic flow makes both ATP and NADPH.\nPhotorespiration uses ATP and makes none.\nThe regeneration stage uses ATP rather than producing it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Blackman (1905)\n(B) Tomatoes and bell pepper in greenhouses\n(C) Tropical plants\n(D) Incident light far beyond saturation\nList-II\n(I) Higher temperature optimum than temperate plants\n(II) Breakdown of chlorophyll and fall in photosynthesis\n(III) Law of Limiting Factors\n(IV) Grown in CO$_2$-enriched atmosphere for higher yield\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Blackman proposed the Law of Limiting Factors in 1905: A-III.\n(B) Tomatoes and bell pepper are C$_3$ crops that respond to extra CO$_2$, so they are grown in CO$_2$-enriched air: B-IV.\n(C) The temperature optimum depends on habitat, and tropical plants have a higher optimum than temperate plants: C-I.\n(D) Increasing incident light beyond a point breaks down chlorophyll and lowers photosynthesis: D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following directly help build the proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane, with high proton concentration in the lumen?\n(i) Splitting of water on the inner side of the membrane\n(ii) The primary acceptor transferring its electron to an H carrier that picks up a proton from the stroma\n(iii) Use of stromal protons by NADP reductase to reduce NADP$^+$\n(iv) Movement of protons through the CF$_0$ channel of ATP synthase\n(v) Conformational change in the CF$_1$ particle","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: protons released by water splitting accumulate in the lumen.\n(ii) Yes: the H carrier removes a proton from the stroma and releases it into the lumen.\n(iii) Yes: NADP reductase on the stroma side uses up stromal protons, lowering their number in the stroma.\n(iv) No: proton flow through CF$_0$ breaks down the gradient.\n(v) No: the CF$_1$ change uses the gradient's energy to make ATP.\nSo three of the five build the gradient.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Within a chloroplast, where do the enzymatic reactions that synthesise sugar, which later forms starch, take place?","image":"","options":["Thylakoid membranes of grana","Stroma (matrix)","Outer membrane of chloroplast","Space between outer and inner membranes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The chloroplast shows a division of labour between its parts.\nThe membrane system (grana and stroma lamellae) traps light energy and makes ATP and NADPH.\nThe enzymatic reactions that build sugar, which then forms starch, occur in the stroma.\nThe thylakoid membranes are a tempting choice, but they carry out the light reactions, not sugar synthesis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The dark reactions of photosynthesis, being enzymatic, are controlled by temperature.\nStatement II: The light reactions of photosynthesis are completely insensitive to temperature.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Dark (carbon) reactions are enzyme-catalysed, so temperature controls them.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says light reactions are also temperature sensitive, only to a much lesser extent.\nThe word 'completely' makes Statement II false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Z scheme, the electrons released by the excited reaction centre of PS II pass through an electron transport system before reaching PS I. This electron transport system consists mainly of:","image":"","options":["Carotenoids","Cytochromes","RuBisCO molecules","CF$_1$ particles of ATP synthase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When P680 absorbs light, its electrons are excited and taken up by an electron acceptor.\nThe acceptor passes them to an electron transport system made of cytochromes, which carries them to PS I.\nCarotenoids are accessory pigments that absorb light. They do not carry electrons between the photosystems.\nRuBisCO is a stroma enzyme of the Calvin cycle. CF$_1$ is the ATP-synthesising part of ATP synthase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-186","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":186,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The action spectrum of photosynthesis shows a complete one-to-one overlap with the absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a.\nStatement II: The action spectrum obtained by Engelmann roughly resembles the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The peaks of the two spectra coincide in the blue and red regions, but the overlap is not complete. Photosynthesis also occurs at wavelengths where chlorophyll a absorbs little.\nStatement II is correct. In Engelmann's experiment, the bacteria gathered mainly in the blue and red light. This roughly matches the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-187","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":187,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"When CO$_2$ is added to PEP, the first stable product synthesised is:","image":"","options":["Pyruvate","Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate","Phosphoglycerate","Oxaloacetate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): In C$_4$ plants, the primary CO$_2$ acceptor in mesophyll cells is phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP), a 3-carbon molecule.\nThe enzyme PEP carboxylase (PEPcase) fixes CO$_2$ onto PEP. This forms oxaloacetic acid (OAA), a 4-carbon acid and the first stable product. That is why the pathway is called C$_4$.\nWhy not (3): 3-phosphoglycerate (PGA) is the first stable product when RuBisCO fixes CO$_2$ onto RuBP in the C$_3$ (Calvin) cycle, not onto PEP.\nPyruvate is formed later, after malate is decarboxylated in bundle sheath cells. G3P is a Calvin cycle intermediate.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 13 (MCQ Q14)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-188","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":188,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the Hatch and Slack pathway, what is the primary $CO_2$ acceptor and how many carbon atoms does it have?","image":"","options":["Ribulose bisphosphate, 5 carbons","Phosphoenol pyruvate, 3 carbons","Oxaloacetic acid, 4 carbons","3-phosphoglyceric acid, 3 carbons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $C_4$ plants, $CO_2$ is first accepted by phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP), a 3-carbon molecule present in the mesophyll cells.\nThe enzyme for this step is PEPcase, and the product is OAA (4C).\nOAA is the first product, not the acceptor.\nRuBP (5C) is the acceptor in $C_3$ plants. PGA is the first product of $C_3$ fixation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-189","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":189,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Synthesis of two molecules of glucose through the Calvin cycle requires 36 ATP and 24 NADPH.\nStatement II: In photorespiration, RuBP combines with $O_2$ to give phosphoglycerate and phosphoglycolate, and ATP is generated in the process.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: each $CO_2$ fixed needs 3 ATP and 2 NADPH. One glucose needs 6 $CO_2$, so it costs 18 ATP and 12 NADPH. Two glucose molecules therefore need 36 ATP and 24 NADPH. This statement is correct.\nStatement II: RuBP + $O_2$ does give one phosphoglycerate and one 2C phosphoglycolate. However, photorespiration makes neither ATP nor NADPH. It actually uses ATP and releases $CO_2$. This statement is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-190","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":190,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) P680\n(b) P700\n(c) Cytochromes\n(d) NADP reductase\nList-II:\n(i) Reaction centre of PS I\n(ii) Reduces NADP$^+$ to NADPH + H$^+$ on the stroma side\n(iii) Reaction centre of PS II\n(iv) Carry electrons downhill from PS II to PS I","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) P680 is the chlorophyll a reaction centre of PS II, with an absorption peak at 680 nm. So (a)-(iii).\n(b) P700 is the reaction centre of PS I, with a peak at 700 nm. So (b)-(i).\n(c) Cytochromes form the electron transport system that carries electrons downhill to PS I. So (c)-(iv).\n(d) NADP reductase sits on the stroma side and reduces NADP$^+$ to NADPH + H$^+$. So (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PHOTOSYNTHESIS-191","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","qno":191,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In non-cyclic electron flow, electrons excited at the reaction centre of PS I finally move downhill to reduce which molecule?","image":"","options":["NADP$^+$, forming NADPH + H$^+$","ADP, forming ATP","O$_2$, forming water","P680, restoring PS II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Electrons excited at P700 pass to an acceptor with a greater redox potential.\nThey then move downhill to NADP$^+$, which is reduced to NADPH + H$^+$.\nADP is phosphorylated using the proton gradient. It does not accept electrons directly.\nP680 gets its electrons back from water splitting, not from PS I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 11 · Photosynthesis in Higher Plants","difficulty":"easy"}]});
